5,10,15]]},"container-title":["2025 3rd International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Automation Control (AIAC)"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.cie.2025.111299","title":["Large language model-driven multi-agent systems for improving production efficiency and reducing carbon emissions in manufacturing"],"author":[{"given":"Tao","family":"Wu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jie","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jinsong","family":"Bao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Qiang","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.cie.2025.111299","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,9]]},"container-title":["Computers &amp; Industrial Engineering"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.eng.2025.10.027","title":["A Large Language Model-based Multi-Agent Framework to Autonomously Design Algorithms for Earth Observation Satellite Scheduling Problem"],"author":[{"given":"Jiawei","family":"Chen","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-7722-5775","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yingguo","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-4236-6235","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Duc Truong","family":"Pham","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4313-8312","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yanjie","family":"Song","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jian","family":"Wu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-6983-4244","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Lining","family":"Xing","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yingwu","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.eng.2025.10.027","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,11]]},"container-title":["Engineering"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.ress.2026.112560","title":["Towards intelligent safety assessment under blast loading: A large language model-powered multi-agent framework"],"author":[{"given":"Lu","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Junbo","family":"Yan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jiang","family":"Yan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yan","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.ress.2026.112560","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,8]]},"container-title":["Reliability Engineering &amp; System Safety"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s11858-026-01796-2","title":["Automated coding of content and pedagogical content knowledge of mathematics using a multi-agent large language model"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-8819-3035","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yasemin","family":"Copur-Gencturk","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-5011-1583","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Kyle","family":"Moreno","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-0072-5455","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yucheng","family":"Chu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-3464-3245","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Hang","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-7125-3898","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jiliang","family":"Tang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s11858-026-01796-2","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,7]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>\n                    Despite the importance of content-specific knowledge for teaching, the task of measuring such knowledge, particularly pedagogical content knowledge (PCK), remains challenging. Scholars have been more successful in capturing the distinct nature of PCK when it has been assessed through open-ended response items, and such responses have been significantly linked to the quality of mathematics instruction and student learning. However, the use of open-ended items entails substantial resource and time demands, as it requires the training and ongoing calibration of human coders to ensure reliable coding of responses. Although scholars have explored technological approaches to automating coding, traditional automated methods have not achieved sufficient reliability for coding complex constructs, such as PCK. Advances in large language models (LLMs) offer new possibilities; however, it remains unclear whether existing LLMs are adequate or whether a purposefully designed LLM framework is required. In this study, we explored the potential of a multi-agent LLM, GradeOpt, which we developed to code mathematics content knowledge (CK) and PCK reliably, in comparison with commonly used automated coding approaches, including two natural language processing (NLP) models (RoBERTa and SBERT) and a widely used LLM (GPT-4o). Using data collected from a national sample of 268 U.S. middle school mathematics teachers who responded to a set of CK and PCK items on ratios and proportional reasoning, we found that the multi-agent LLM, GradeOpt, achieved substantial agreement overall (\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:alternatives>\n                        <jats:tex-math>$\\kappa = .68$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                        <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\">\n                          <mml:mi>\u03ba<\/mml:mi>\n                          <mml:mo>=<\/mml:mo>\n                          <mml:mo>.<\/mml:mo>\n                          <mml:mn>68<\/mml:mn>\n                        <\/mml:math>\n                      <\/jats:alternatives>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    ; weighted\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:alternatives>\n                        <jats:tex-math>$\\kappa = .79$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                        <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\">\n                          <mml:mi>\u03ba<\/mml:mi>\n                          <mml:mo>=<\/mml:mo>\n                          <mml:mo>.<\/mml:mo>\n                          <mml:mn>79<\/mml:mn>\n                        <\/mml:math>\n                      <\/jats:alternatives>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    ) and substantially outperformed the comparison models (\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:alternatives>\n                        <jats:tex-math>$\\kappa \\leq .39$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                        <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\">\n                          <mml:mi>\u03ba<\/mml:mi>\n                          <mml:mo>\u2264<\/mml:mo>\n                          <mml:mo>.<\/mml:mo>\n                          <mml:mn>39<\/mml:mn>\n                        <\/mml:math>\n                      <\/jats:alternatives>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    ; weighted\n                    <jats:inline-formula>\n                      <jats:alternatives>\n                        <jats:tex-math>$\\kappa \\leq .55$<\/jats:tex-math>\n                        <mml:math xmlns:mml=\"http:\/\/www.w3.org\/1998\/Math\/MathML\">\n                          <mml:mi>\u03ba<\/mml:mi>\n                          <mml:mo>\u2264<\/mml:mo>\n                          <mml:mo>.<\/mml:mo>\n                          <mml:mn>55<\/mml:mn>\n                        <\/mml:math>\n                      <\/jats:alternatives>\n                    <\/jats:inline-formula>\n                    ) on PCK items. These results demonstrate that a multi-agent LLM framework has the potential to code open-ended responses at a level comparable to human coders. We discuss the implications for advancing automated assessment in teacher education and mathematics education.\n                  <\/jats:p>","container-title":["ZDM \u2013 Mathematics Education"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.compind.2025.104399","title":["Personalized safety training for construction workers: A large language model-driven multi-agent framework integrated with knowledge graph reasoning"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-4701-4533","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Qihua","family":"Chen","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-8270-8632","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xianfei","family":"Yin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Beifei","family":"Yuan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Qirong","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.compind.2025.104399","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,1]]},"container-title":["Computers in Industry"]},{"DOI":"10.21123\/2411-7986.5338","title":["Architecting Reliable Multi-Agent Systems with Large Language Models Through Model-Driven Engineering"],"author":[{"given":"Najma Imtiaz","family":"Ali","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Fakulti Teknologi Maklumat dan Komunikasi, Universiti Teknikal Malaysia Melaka, Malaysia AND Institute of Mathematics and Computer Science, University of Sindh, Jamshoro, Sindh, Pakistan"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Aadil","family":"Jamali","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Institute of Mathematics and Computer Science, University of Sindh, Jamshoro, Sindh, Pakistan"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Imtiaz Ali","family":"Brohi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Fakulti Teknologi Maklumat dan Komunikasi, Universiti Teknikal Malaysia Melaka, Malaysia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Noor Zaman","family":"Jhanjhi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer Science, Faculty of Innovation & Technology, Taylor's University, Malaysia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Emaliana","family":"Kasmuri","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Center for Advanced Computing Technology, Universiti Teknikal Malaysia Melaka, Malaysia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21123\/2411-7986.5338","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,26]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Multi-agent systems based on large language models (LLM) are said to be able to provide flexible and scalable collaboration, but are often unstable, adversarially active, biased in their representations, and expensive to compute - aspects that hinder their use in safety-critical or resource-sensitive systems. To overcome these deficiencies, a model-driven engineering framework, the MDE-Agentware, has been implemented: a model-based engineering paradigm, which represents multi-agent architectures through a typed domain specific metamodel, and generates executables that are conforming with instrumented runtime monitoring, tool wrappers and energy-conscious invocation policies. The main novelties of the approach include (i) a formal model-to-code transformation, enforcing the architectural constraints and labeled-transition semantics, (ii) a collection of rigorous metrics (architectural conformity, prediction consistency, composite bias penalty, and energy models), which allow performing automated conformity checking and constrained optimization, and (iii) fairness-by-design and resilience mechanisms, implemented into the orchestration level, and not applied after the fact. Huge controlled experiments with publicly available corpora and simulated benchmarks show that MDE-Agentware achieves significantly better contraction behavior and spectral condition, is more resistant to adversarial noise, has higher inter-agent coherence, exhibits significant reductions in redundant invocations of LLM and per-trial energy, and significant reductions in statistical parity difference. The framework thus progresses a viable, repeatable, and reliable, and environmentally sustainable multi-agent system based on LLM, which can be utilized in critical applications.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Baghdad Science Journal"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.cities.2026.107232","title":["A multi-agent large language model workflow for analyzing perceived cultural values from social media: A study of 141 Chinese cities"],"author":[{"given":"Xukai","family":"Zhao","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yuxing","family":"Lu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"He","family":"Huang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Guanhong","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Chenxi","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.cities.2026.107232","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,8]]},"container-title":["Cities"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/icsft66733.2026.11507464","title":["Multi-Agent Framework for Interactive Storytelling using Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"S.","family":"Lalitha","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Vel Tech Rangarajan Dr. Sagunthala R&amp;D Institute of Science and Technology,Computer Science and Engineering (Artificial Intelligence And Machine Learning),Chennai,India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"P.Karthik","family":"Bharawaj","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Vel Tech Rangarajan Dr. Sagunthala R&amp;D Institute of Science and Technology,Computer Science and Engineering (Artificial Intelligence And Machine Learning),Chennai,India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"P.Manikanta","family":"Prasad","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Vel Tech Rangarajan Dr. Sagunthala R&amp;D Institute of Science and Technology,Computer Science and Engineering (Artificial Intelligence And Machine Learning),Chennai,India"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/icsft66733.2026.11507464","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,1,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,1,2]]},"container-title":["2026 International Conference on Smart Futuristic Technology"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6080813","title":["Balancing Comfort and Cost: A Multi-Agent Framework for Human-Centric Building Energy Management via Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Deli","family":"Liu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Xiaoping","family":"Zhou","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Yu","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6080813","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]}},{"DOI":"10.1109\/mrs66243.2025.11357269","title":["Compositional Coordination for Multi-Robot Teams with Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Zhehui","family":"Huang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Southern California,Department of Computer Science,Los Angeles,CA,USA,90089"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Guangyao","family":"Shi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Southern California,Department of Computer Science,Los Angeles,CA,USA,90089"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yuwei","family":"Wu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Pennsylvania,GRASP Lab,Philadelphia,PA,USA,19104"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Vijay","family":"Kumar","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Pennsylvania,GRASP Lab,Philadelphia,PA,USA,19104"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Gaurav S.","family":"Sukhatme","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Southern California,Department of Computer Science,Los Angeles,CA,USA,90089"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/mrs66243.2025.11357269","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,4]]},"container-title":["2025 IEEE International Symposium on Multi-Robot and Multi-Agent Systems (MRS)"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/eiecc67963.2025.11409558","title":["Contextual Trust Evaluation for Robust Coordination in Large Language Model Multi-Agent Systems"],"author":[{"given":"Kangning","family":"Gao","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"The George Washington University,Washington,DC,USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Haotian","family":"Zhu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"New York University,New York,USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Rui","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Leeds,Leeds,United Kingdom"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jinming","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Georgia Institute of Technology,Atlanta,USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xu","family":"Yan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Trine University,Phoenix,USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yi","family":"Hu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Southern California,Los Angeles,USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/eiecc67963.2025.11409558","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,26]]},"container-title":["2025 5th International Conference on Electronic Information Engineering and Computer Communication (EIECC)"]},{"DOI":"10.24963\/ijcai.2025\/12","title":["L2M2: A Hierarchical Framework Integrating Large Language Model and Multi-agent Reinforcement Learning"],"author":[{"given":"Minghong","family":"Geng","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Singapore Management University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Shubham","family":"Pateria","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Singapore Management University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Budhitama","family":"Subagdja","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Singapore Management University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Lin","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"MIGU Co., Ltd"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xin","family":"Zhao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Tsinghua University, Beijing National Research Center for Information Science and Technology"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ah-Hwee","family":"Tan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Singapore Management University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.24963\/ijcai.2025\/12","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,9]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) has demonstrated remarkable success in collaborative tasks, yet faces significant challenges in scaling to complex scenarios requiring sustained planning and coordination across long horizons. While hierarchical approaches help decompose these tasks, they typically rely on hand-crafted subtasks and domain-specific knowledge, limiting their generalizability. We present L2M2, a novel hierarchical framework that leverages large language models (LLMs) for high-level strategic planning and MARL for low-level execution. L2M2 enables zero-shot planning that supports both end-to-end training and direct integration with pre-trained MARL models. Experiments in the VMAS environment demonstrate that L2M2's LLM-guided MARL achieves superior performance while requiring less than 20% of the training samples compared to baseline methods. In the MOSMAC environment, L2M2 demonstrates strong performance with pre-defined subgoals and maintains substantial effectiveness without subgoals - scenarios where baseline methods consistently fail. Analysis through kernel density estimation reveals L2M2's ability to automatically generate appropriate navigation plans, demonstrating its potential for addressing complex multi-agent coordination tasks.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Proceedings of the Thirty-Fourth International Joint Conference on Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.aei.2025.103527","title":["MASC: Large language model-based multi-agent scheduling chain for flexible job shop scheduling problem"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-5121-511X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zelong","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-9281-5925","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Chenhui","family":"Wan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0750-1030","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jie","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2490-5440","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xi","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Haifeng","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Youmin","family":"Hu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-8236-7903","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zhongxu","family":"Hu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.aei.2025.103527","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,9]]},"container-title":["Advanced Engineering Informatics"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/icassp55912.2026.11460555","title":["N2CDrive: Negotiate to Cooperate for Multi-Agent Autonomous Driving via Large Vision-Language Model"],"author":[{"given":"Xingpeng","family":"Li","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Beijing University of Posts and Telecommunications (BUPT),Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Enwen","family":"Hu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Beijing University of Posts and Telecommunications (BUPT),Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Jingrong","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Science and Technology Beijing (USTB),Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Siyuan","family":"Jin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Beijing University of Posts and Telecommunications (BUPT),Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Huirong","family":"Cai","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Beijing University of Posts and Telecommunications (BUPT),Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/icassp55912.2026.11460555","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,3]]},"container-title":["ICASSP 2026 - 2026 IEEE International Conference on Acoustics, Speech and Signal Processing (ICASSP)"]},{"DOI":"10.71465\/gmssrj167","title":["Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning for Cooperative Large Language Model Collaboration"],"author":[{"family":"Thomas J. Bennett","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"family":"Samuel K. O\u2019Neill","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"family":"Laura M. Harding","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.71465\/gmssrj167","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,10]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>This study investigates cooperative LLM collaboration through a multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) framework, where agents are jointly optimized using a shared group reward rather than independent objectives. A multi-agent GRPO (MAGRPO) algorithm is proposed to stabilize learning under decentralized execution. Evaluation is performed on 4 collaborative benchmarks covering reasoning, writing, and code synthesis, totaling 5,200 training episodes. Results show that MAGRPO improves average task reward by 27.6%, reduces inter-agent contradiction rates by 31.2%, and accelerates convergence by 1.8\u00d7 compared to independent RL fine-tuning.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Global Media and Social Sciences Research Journal"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.jcrimjus.2026.102692","title":["Detecting cyber employment fraud in platform-based space: A multi-agent large language model approach"],"author":[{"given":"Quan","family":"Sun","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ling","family":"Wu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xinyue","family":"Ye","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Claire","family":"Lee","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Wei","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Cuiling","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.jcrimjus.2026.102692","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,7]]},"container-title":["Journal of Criminal Justice"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s10822-026-00834-1","title":["Target-aware molecule SMILES generation using a large language model with retrieval-augmented generation, multi-turn memory, and a predictive model"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-8072-2104","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Piotr","family":"Karabowicz","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Rados\u0142aw","family":"Charkiewicz","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Alicja","family":"Charkiewicz","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jacek","family":"Nikli\u0144ski","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s10822-026-00834-1","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,29]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,29]]},"container-title":["Journal of Computer-Aided Molecular Design"]},{"DOI":"10.20944\/preprints202511.0840.v1","title":["Multi-Modal Large Language Model for Medical Auxiliary Diagnosis"],"author":[{"given":"Ziyu","family":"Fang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Minghao","family":"Ye","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.20944\/preprints202511.0840.v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,13]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The application of language models in medicine faces persistent challenges, including outdated knowledge, factual hallucination, and limited integration of multi-modal clinical data. To overcome these issues, this study presents DiagnostiCare, a multi-modal enhanced intelligent diagnostic system designed for medical auxiliary diagnosis. The framework combines a core language understanding model with specialized modules for real-time retrieval (MedRAG), structured knowledge reasoning (MedKG), multi-modal data interpretation (Tools module), and comprehensive information access (Search module). A challenging dataset of complex medical questions was developed to evaluate system performance through expert-based assessments. Experimental results show that DiagnostiCare achieves superior accuracy, treatment relevance, and information reliability compared with general-purpose models. Ablation studies highlight the essential role of each module, particularly in managing multi-modal information. These results demonstrate the potential of a modular, knowledge-driven approach for developing safe and trustworthy AI systems in clinical practice.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.geomat.2025.100071","title":["Multi-agent systems of large language models as weight assigners: An approach to collaborative weighting in spatial multi-criteria decision-making"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-7922-715X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Mohammad H.","family":"Vahidnia","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.geomat.2025.100071","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,12]]},"container-title":["Geomatica"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/raai67517.2025.11423310","title":["Facilitating Shared Introspection in Hierarchical Multi-Agent Systems Using the Model Context Protocol"],"author":[{"given":"Krishna","family":"Arun","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Eastlake High School,Sammamish,United States"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Paras","family":"Goel","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Columbia University,New York,United States"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/raai67517.2025.11423310","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,18]]},"container-title":["2025 5th International Conference on Robotics, Automation, and Artificial Intelligence (RAAI)"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/icdmw69685.2025.00306","title":["Dynamic, Efficient, and Generalizable Multi-Agent Communication for Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Song","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Virginia,Charlottesville,USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Jundong","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Virginia,Charlottesville,USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/icdmw69685.2025.00306","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,12]]},"container-title":["2025 IEEE International Conference on Data Mining Workshops (ICDMW)"]},{"DOI":"10.1038\/s41598-025-23904-0","title":["A multi agent classical Chinese translation method based on large language models"],"author":[{"given":"Weifeng","family":"Lv","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Qiong","family":"Cao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xiaoyang","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1038\/s41598-025-23904-0","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,17]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,17]]},"container-title":["Scientific Reports"]},{"DOI":"10.26434\/chemrxiv.15003915\/v1","title":["PVmatAgent: A Large Language Model (LLM) Agent for Photovoltaic and Perovskite Material Design and Analysis"],"author":[{"given":"Hangyuan","family":"Deng","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Chemistry and Material Science","place":["Nanjing, China"]},{"name":"Nanjing University of Information Science & Technology","place":["Nanjing, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yuyan","family":"Wu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Software","place":["Nanjing, China"]},{"name":"Nanjing University of Information Science & Technology","place":["Nanjing, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Youle","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Software","place":["Nanjing, China"]},{"name":"Nanjing University of Information Science & Technology","place":["Nanjing, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6873-7314","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Lei","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Chemistry and Material Science","place":["Nanjing, China"]},{"name":"Nanjing University of Information Science & Technology","place":["Nanjing, China"]},{"name":"School of Software","place":["Nanjing, China"]},{"name":"Nanjing University of Information Science & Technology","place":["Nanjing, China"]},{"name":"Key Laboratory of Spectral Imaging Technology","place":["Xi\u2019an, China"]},{"name":"Xi\u2019an Institute of Optics and Precision Mechanics of the Chinese Academy of Sciences","place":["Xi\u2019an, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.26434\/chemrxiv.15003915\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,26]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The rapid advancement of large language models (LLMs) has opened new opportunities for materials informatics. However, LLMs fall short in photovoltaic (PV) material design due to their lack of domain grounding, unreliable outputs, and inability to perform integrated computational tasks. To address these issues, this work presents PVmatAgent, an autonomous LLM-based computational agent designed specifically for PV materials design and analysis. PVmatAgent implements 13 domain-specific tools, falling into 9 functional modules or four categories at a higher level: knowledge acquisition, physics simulation, performance evaluation, and experimental guidance. The system integrates over a dozen domain-specific tools, including the machine learning force field CHGNet for geometric relaxation and formation energy calculation, the graph neural network MEGNet for bandgap prediction, the Goldschmidt tolerance factor and octahedral factor for perovskite stability screening, the photovoltaic performance evaluator including the Shockley\u2013Queisser limit, SLME model and tandem current matching module, the material project agent for structure retrieval, the retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) knowledge base. A hallucination truncation mechanism prevents the model from fabricating numerical outputs. The system was validated on four representative scenarios. Results demonstrate that PVmatAgent effectively executes computational tasks and provides corrective recommendations grounded in literature evidence for photovoltaic material design. This work offers a practical paradigm for LLM-based autonomous agents in AI-driven optoelectronic materials discovery.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1109\/icndsa68777.2026.11652131","title":["Towards Stable AI-Driven Equity Valuation Using Multi-Agent Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Jatin","family":"Chopra","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"George Washington University","place":["Washington, DC, USA"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/icndsa68777.2026.11652131","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,4]]},"container-title":["2026 International Conference on NextGen Data Science and Analytics (ICNDSA)"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7083227","title":["MeshExpert: A Physics-Aware Large Language Model Agent Framework for Automated Industrial FE Meshing"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9401-9575","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Rui","family":"Zhao","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yiming","family":"Han","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-4548-308X","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Guannan","family":"Tian","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Tinglun","family":"Song","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7083227","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>While Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate strong capabilities in general-purpose tasks, their application in specialized engineering domains such as Computer-Aided Engineering (CAE) is constrained by a lack of domain knowledge and physical awareness. In tasks like finite element (FE) mesh generation, the model&amp;apos;s operational sequences must not only be logically executable but also satisfy specific geometric and physical quality metrics. This paper presents MeshExpert, a physics-aware domain LLM agent framework for automated CAE preprocessing. Using FE meshing as a representative instance, the framework first fine-tunes a 20B-parameter foundation model on a curated dataset of API semantics and expert workflows to build a domain-specific knowledge base. Subsequently, Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) is applied to align the model with physical constraints. The reward mechanism is directly formulated based on actual simulation feedback, integrating task executability, physical mesh quality, and operational efficiency. During inference, a Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) module is utilized to access domain engineering guidelines. Experiments on a benchmark of 50 industrial automotive components show that MeshExpert achieves an 82.5% Pass@3 execution rate and an average Jacobian of 0.78. Compared to existing baseline models, the proposed framework improves task completion rates and the physical validity of the generated models, providing a practical technical framework for applying domain LLMs in physics-constrained engineering tasks.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3748522.3779794","title":["Adaptive Multi-User Scheduling for Large Language Model Inference on HBM-Based Processing-in-Memory Accelerators"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-7208-9396","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Chen-Lin","family":"Shi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"National Taiwan University of Science and Technology, Taipei, Taiwan, Taiwan"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-2504-723X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xi-Zhu","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"National Taiwan University of Science and Technology, Taipei, Taiwan, Taiwan"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-8698-1318","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ya-Shu","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Electrical Engineering, National Taiwan University of Science and Technology, Taipei, Taiwan"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3748522.3779794","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,23]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,23]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 41st ACM\/SIGAPP Symposium on Applied Computing"]},{"DOI":"10.38007\/ml.2026.060103","title":["Research on a Robotic Natural Language Intelligent Decision-Making Framework Based on Large Language Models, Thinking Chain Reasoning, and Multi-Agent Collaboration"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.38007\/ml.2026.060103","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,10]]},"container-title":["Machine Learning Theory and Practice"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/978-981-95-3001-4_5","title":["A Large Language Model Agent-Guided Multi-agent System for\u00a0Adaptive Traffic Signal Control"],"author":[{"given":"Minglu","family":"Zhu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-5319-4599","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Congcong","family":"Zhu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-981-95-3001-4_5","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,17]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,17]]},"container-title":["Lecture Notes in Computer Science","Knowledge Science, Engineering and Management"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/isctis70043.2026.11572386","title":["Natural Language to Maritime Insights: A Large Language Model Agent Solution"],"author":[{"given":"Zice","family":"Qiu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Sun Yat-sen University,School of Geospatial Engineering and Science,Zhuhai,China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xiang","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Sun Yat-sen University,School of Geospatial Engineering and Science,Zhuhai,China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Donghua","family":"Yu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Sun Yat-sen University,School of Geospatial Engineering and Science,Zhuhai,China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/isctis70043.2026.11572386","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,15]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,15]]},"container-title":["2026 6th International Symposium on Computer Technology and Information Science (ISCTIS)"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/tsg.2026.3672814","title":["Low Carbon Scheduling of Integrated Energy System Based on Large Language Model-Embedded Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning"],"author":[{"given":"Chenyue","family":"Xia","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Shenzhen International Graduate School (SIGS), Tsinghua University, Shenzhen, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Tong","family":"Gou","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Shenzhen International Graduate School (SIGS), Tsinghua University, Shenzhen, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-5149-5101","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yinliang","family":"Xu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Shenzhen International Graduate School (SIGS), Tsinghua University, Shenzhen, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5268-5289","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ye","family":"Guo","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Building Environment and Energy Engineering, Hong Kong Polytechnic University, Kowloon, Hong Kong, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5465-9818","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Hongbin","family":"Sun","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Tsinghua University, Beijing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/tsg.2026.3672814","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["IEEE Transactions on Smart Grid"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/compsac69091.2026.00095","title":["PentestLLM: A Cyber Kill Chain-driven Multi-Agent Large Language Model Framework Enabling Automatic Penetration Testing"],"author":[{"given":"Fei","family":"Du","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Liverpool","place":["Liverpool, UK"],"department":["Department of Computer Science"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Hiroyuki","family":"Sato","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"National Institute of Informatics","place":["Tokyo, Japan"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zhenzhen","family":"Jiang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Advanced Technology, Xi'an Jiaotong-Liverpool University","place":["Suzhou, Jiangsu, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Lekun","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Advanced Technology, Xi'an Jiaotong-Liverpool University","place":["Suzhou, Jiangsu, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yang","family":"Hong","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Advanced Technology, Xi'an Jiaotong-Liverpool University","place":["Suzhou, Jiangsu, China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yan","family":"Bai","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Engineering and Technology, University of Washington","place":["Tacoma, WA, USA"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Wenjun","family":"Fan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Engineering and Technology, University of Washington","place":["Tacoma, WA, USA"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/compsac69091.2026.00095","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,7]]},"container-title":["2026 IEEE 50th Annual Computers, Software, and Applications Conference (COMPSAC)"]},{"DOI":"10.1080\/17452007.2026.2647805","title":["Large language model-driven multi-agent framework for fault detection and diagnostics of variable air volume boxes"],"author":[{"given":"Deli","family":"Liu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Beijing University of Civil Engineering and Architecture","place":["Beijing, People\u2019s Republic of China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xiaoping","family":"Zhou","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Beijing University of Civil Engineering and Architecture","place":["Beijing, People\u2019s Republic of China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yu","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Beijing University of Civil Engineering and Architecture","place":["Beijing, People\u2019s Republic of China"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1080\/17452007.2026.2647805","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,4]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,21]]},"container-title":["Architectural Engineering and Design Management"]},{"DOI":"10.1186\/s12909-026-10169-y","title":["A 9\u2009+\u20091 multi-agent collaborative teaching model for clinical reasoning: integrating large language models into medical education"],"author":[{"given":"Qian","family":"Dong","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jing","family":"Chang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Cheng","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jing","family":"Wu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Panpan","family":"Feng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Rui","family":"Feng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Guoli","family":"Yang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Min","family":"Mao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1186\/s12909-026-10169-y","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,18]]},"container-title":["BMC Medical Education"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6075812","title":["Retail-Informed Secure Wholesale Energy Market and Bid Generation with Projection Clearing Exchange for Networked Microgrids: A Large Language Model-Assisted Multi-agent Deep Reinforcement Learning Approach"],"author":[{"given":"Reza","family":"Sepehrzad","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Milad","family":"Moafi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Nima","family":"Khosravi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"HOSSEIN","family":"HOSSEINALIBEIKI","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Ahmed","family":"Al durra","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Mahdieh S.","family":"Sadabadi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6075812","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]}},{"DOI":"10.36227\/techrxiv.177160638.89229642\/v1","title":["A Survey on Large Language Model-based Multi-Agent Systems: Paradigms, Applications, and Challenges"],"author":[{"given":"Mingjie","family":"Zhu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mingcheng","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mingzhang","family":"Cui","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ziyun","family":"Qian","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yue","family":"Jiang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Minghao","family":"Han","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mingyang","family":"Sun","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zizhi","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-0849-5027","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yizhou","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yang","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jinghang","family":"Han","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Peng","family":"Zhai","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Dingkang","family":"Yang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Lihua","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36227\/techrxiv.177160638.89229642\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,20]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>With the rapid advancement of Large Language Models (LLM), artificial intelligence is undergoing a paradigm shift from solitary intelligence to collaborative Multi-Agent Systems (MAS). By simulating the collaborative division of labor found in human societies, LLM-based Multi-Agent Systems (LLM-MAS) effectively overcome the performance bottlenecks inherent in single-agent models when addressing complex tasks. Addressing the prevailing fragmentation and the absence of a unified taxonomy in current research, this paper introduces a three-dimensional analytical framework integrating Structure, Interaction, and Evolution. We systematically elucidate the evolutionary logic of organizational architectures from static presets to dynamic adaptation; deeply dissect the underlying mechanisms of communication paradigms and collective decision-making; and explore pathways to self-evolution through prompt restructuring, memory consolidation, and parameter updates. Furthermore, we categorize cutting-edge applications into three domains: \"AI for Humans\", \"AI for the World\", and \"AI for AI\". Finally, we analyze critical challenges, including hallucination propagation and safety governance, providing clear theoretical support and a technical roadmap for advancing the field toward artificial general intelligence.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.seppur.2026.137462","title":["A large language model-based multi-agent methodology for intelligent materials screening: A case study on MOFs for CO2 capture"],"author":[{"given":"Yuanming","family":"Li","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jie","family":"Zhao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xiangkun Elvis","family":"Cao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Shuangjun","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.seppur.2026.137462","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,7]]},"container-title":["Separation and Purification Technology"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.aei.2025.103888","title":["A Large language model-based multi-agent manufacturing system for intelligent shopfloors"],"author":[{"given":"Zhen","family":"Zhao","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Dunbing","family":"Tang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Changchun","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Liping","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zequn","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Haihua","family":"Zhu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Kai","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Qingwei","family":"Nie","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yuchen","family":"Ji","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.aei.2025.103888","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,1]]},"container-title":["Advanced Engineering Informatics"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s11432-026-4986-x","title":["Large language model-based multi-agent systems for financial markets simulation: a survey"],"author":[{"given":"Qinyuan","family":"Liu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Lihang","family":"Yao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zidong","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yufan","family":"Yang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yifei","family":"Tang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Dawei","family":"Cheng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Changjun","family":"Jiang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s11432-026-4986-x","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,7]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,24]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,24]]},"container-title":["Science China Information Sciences"]},{"DOI":"10.2196\/preprints.102343","title":["Evaluating a Large Language Model-Based Multi-Agent System (Breast-COSMA) for Post-Surgical Breast Cancer Rehabilitation: A Pilot Randomized Controlled Trial (Preprint)"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-5157-5334","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zikang","family":"Chen","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Tan","family":"Xie","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Qiujing","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jinyan","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xudong","family":"Lv","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Heming","family":"Zheng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Qinchuan","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2196\/preprints.102343","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,25]]},"abstract":"<sec>\n                  <title>BACKGROUND<\/title>\n                  <p>Post-surgical breast cancer rehabilitation requires tailored guidance. Current mHealth tools offer generalized protocols and lack empathetic interaction, leading to poor patient adherence.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                <sec>\n                  <title>OBJECTIVE<\/title>\n                  <p>This study evaluates the feasibility, usability, and clinical efficacy of Breast-COSMA, an LLM-based multi-agent system designed for personalized out-of-hospital rehabilitation.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                <sec>\n                  <title>METHODS<\/title>\n                  <p>We developed Breast-COSMA, a system deployed via a WeChat Mini-program using a collaborative multi-agent framework integrated with fine-tuning and retrieval-augmented generation. A 6-week pilot randomized controlled trial was conducted with 50 postoperative breast cancer patients assigned to either Breast-COSMA (n=29) or routine management (n=21). Primary outcomes included shoulder Range of Motion (ROM); secondary outcomes included physical well-being (FACT-B) and upper limb disability (Quick-DASH).<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                <sec>\n                  <title>RESULTS<\/title>\n                  <p>Thirty patients (15 per group) completed the trial. Breast-COSMA demonstrated excellent usability, with 79.8% of patient interactions seamlessly completed via speech, lowering barriers for patients with restricted limb movement. Over 6 weeks, the system achieved a high follow-up compliance rate of 96.7%. Clinically, the intervention group showed significantly faster recovery in upper limb function, with a substantial reduction in Quick-DASH scores compared to controls (P &lt; .001). Significant time x group interaction effects were observed in shoulder ROM extension (52.14\u00b0 vs. 48.64\u00b0; P = .02), total ROM (531.07 vs. 501.71; P = .03), and FACT-B physical well-being (24.80 vs. 21.86; P = .008).<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                <sec>\n                  <title>CONCLUSIONS<\/title>\n                  <p>Breast-COSMA successfully transitions LLM multi-agent technology into a validated clinical tool. It provides a feasible, engaging, and effective approach to out-of-hospital rehabilitation, significantly improving functional recovery and patient adherence.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                <sec>\n                  <title>CLINICALTRIAL<\/title>\n                  <p>chictr.org.cn ChiCTR2600124771<\/p>\n                <\/sec>"},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3807246.3807341","title":["A Multi-Agent Large Language Model Framework for Marketing Decision-Making with Auditable Attribution Analysis"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-2365-0045","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Qianxi","family":"Liu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of South Florida, Tampa, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-3557-0115","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Hengguang","family":"Cui","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Brown University, Providence, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-7258-1849","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yingzi","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Minnesota, Minneapolis, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-0730-1386","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Chi Shing","family":"Lee","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Hunter College, New York, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-3995-152X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Sheng","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Northeastern University, Seattle, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-5004-758X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Linghao","family":"Yang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Chicago, Chicago, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3807246.3807341","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,6]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,6]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 2026 International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Control"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.asoc.2025.113625","title":["DAPlanner: Dual-agent framework with multi-modal large language model for autonomous driving motion planning"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-3279-1488","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Pin","family":"Zhang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-3429-5877","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ke","family":"Lin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2902-7365","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Duantengchuan","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-6699-9697","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zixun","family":"Fu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-4316-1096","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Yuefeng","family":"Cai","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Bing","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-5733-3432","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Huan","family":"Yu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ming","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.asoc.2025.113625","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,11]]},"container-title":["Applied Soft Computing"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6273819","title":["SuperLocalMemory: Privacy-Preserving Multi-Agent Memory with Bayesian Trust Defense Against Memory Poisoning"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-8726-4289","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Varun Pratap","family":"Bhardwaj","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6273819","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>We present SuperLocalMemory, a local-first memory system for multi-agent AI that defends against OWASP ASI06 memory poisoning through architectural isolation and Bayesian trust scoring, while personalizing retrieval through adaptive learning-to-rank-all without cloud dependencies or LLM inference calls. As AI agents increasingly rely on persistent memory, cloud-based memory systems create centralized attack surfaces where poisoned memories propagate across sessions and users-a threat demonstrated in documented attacks against production systems. Our architecture combines SQLite-backed storage with FTS5 full-text search, Leiden-based knowledge graph clustering, an event-driven coordination layer with per-agent provenance, and an adaptive re-ranking framework that learns user preferences through three-layer behavioral analysis (cross-project technology preferences, project context detection, and workflow pattern mining). Evaluation across seven benchmark dimensions demonstrates 10.6ms median search latency, zero concurrency errors under 10 simultaneous agents, trust separation (gap = 0.90) with 72% trust degradation for sleeper attacks, and 104% improvement in NDCG@5 when adaptive re-ranking is enabled. Behavioral data is isolated in a separate database supporting GDPR Article 17 erasure requests via one-command deletion. SuperLocalMemory is open-source (MIT) and integrates with 17+ development tools via Model Context Protocol.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6906675","title":["Self-Improving Alpha Mining for Quantitative Trading via Multi-Agent Large Language Models with Knowledge Base Accumulation"],"author":[{"given":"Son  Minh","family":"Vu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Trung  The","family":"Pham","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4675-2123","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Viet  Hong","family":"Tran","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6906675","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Quantitative trading strategies depend on discovering novel alpha factors that capture market\u00a0inefficiencies, yet traditional approaches rely on manual feature engineering, which is time consuming and requires substantial domain expertise. This paper proposes an automated framework that leverages large language models (LLMs) to systematically generate, refine, and\u00a0evaluate trading alpha strategies through a multi-agent pipeline comprising a WriterAgent, JudgeAgent, and BacktestEngine, organized in a nested-loop architecture. The key contribution of this work is the systematic integration of WorldQuant 101 Formulaic Alphas as\u00a0a structured knowledge prior, rooted in proven formulaic patterns rather than unconstrained\u00a0free-form synthesis. This design significantly narrows the search space, reduces invalid code\u00a0generation, and accelerates convergence of the refinement loop. Experiments on Vietnamese\u00a0stock market data demonstrate that the proposed framework consistently generates alpha factors\u00a0with statistically significant Information Coefficient (IC) values and positive Sharpe ratios\u00a0within a short generation time. Furthermore, the incorporation of WorldQuant 101 Formulaic\u00a0Alphas consistently outperforms unconstrained LLM-based generation in both alpha quality\u00a0and convergence efficiency, establishing a scalable, robust, and domain-grounded approach for\u00a0automated alpha mining.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.71465\/csb224","title":["Language-Driven Coordination in Multi-Agent Reinforcement\u00a0Learning with Large Language Models"],"author":[{"family":"Benjamin J. Wilson","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"family":"Oliver Smith","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"family":"Chloe A. Thompson","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.71465\/csb224","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,1,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,1,30]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>This study integrates LLMs into MARL through language-based coordination, communication, and memory modules. The framework combines proximal policy optimization (PPO) with language-conditioned subgoal generation. Experiments across three multi-agent environments involving over 10,000 training episodes show a 14.7\u201326.3% increase in win rate compared with MAPPO and QMIX baselines, with ablation results confirming the contribution of languageguided coordination to sample efficiency and zero-shot generalization.\u00a0<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Computer Science Bulletin"]},{"DOI":"10.5220\/0014301900004058","title":["NOMAD: A Multi-Agent LLM System for UML Class Diagram Generation from Natural Language Requirements"],"author":[{"given":"Polydoros","family":"Giannouris","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"The University of Manchester, U.K."}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Sophia","family":"Ananiadou","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"The University of Manchester, U.K."}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5220\/0014301900004058","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 14th International Conference on Model-Based Software and Systems Engineering"]},{"DOI":"10.21203\/rs.3.rs-9960161\/v1","title":["How Well Do Large Language Models Agree with Humans \u2014 and Each Other? A Multi-Model, Multi-Domain Sentiment Benchmark Study"],"author":[{"given":"Aneesh K","family":"Sajan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21203\/rs.3.rs-9960161\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,23]]},"abstract":"<title>Abstract<\/title>\n                <p>Large language models (LLMs) are now widely used to classify sentiment. Yet we rarely compare models from different providers on the same data, and we know little about how often they agree with each other. We evaluate five LLMs from four providers (Claude Opus 4.7, GPT-4o, GPT-5.5, Llama 3.1 8B, and Gemini 2.5 Pro) and add VADER, a rule based lexicon, as a baseline. The test set has five human annotated sentiment datasets and 8,872 sentences, drawn from formal and informal text in financial and general domains, scored with one fixed zero-shot prompt. We report Macro F1 and Cohen\u2019s \u03ba against human labels, agreement between every pair of models, and how often the models reach consensus. Claude Opus 4.7 and GPT-5.5 score highest (mean Macro F1 0.775 and 0.776; mean \u03ba against humans 0.690 and 0.700). With GPT-4o they form a tight cluster (\u03ba 0.74 to 0.89). Llama is moderate but open and can run locally. Gemini 2.5 Pro scores near chance. We test the \u201coverthinking\u201d explanation by re-running it at the smallest thinking budget the model allows; the score does not change, so overthinking is not the cause. The six models all agree on only 14 to 24 percent of sentences. Model choice therefore matters: two people using different LLMs on the same text can get different sentiment, especially on informal data. We also find that \u201cneutral\u201d means different things in different datasets, which limits cross- domain comparison of neutral scores. We release all predictions, code, and data.<\/p>"},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3770855.3818940","title":["Large Language Model (LLM) as an Excellent Reinforcement Learning Researcher in both Single-Agent and Multi-Agent Scenarios"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-9204-3932","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Vincent","family":"Fu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Hedge Fund, Beijing, China and Peking University, Beijing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-8683-8318","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xinxin","family":"Xu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Software and Microelectronics, Peking University, Beijing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-9486-1693","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Weichen","family":"Xu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Peking University, Beijing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-2017-2159","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ruilong","family":"Ren","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Huawei Technologies Ltd., Shanghai, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-5140-7237","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Bowen","family":"Deng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Software and Microelectronics, Peking University, Beijing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-7304-1587","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jue","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Alibaba Group, Beijing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-2665-6155","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xinyu","family":"Zhao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Peking University, Beijing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4724-7065","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jian","family":"Cao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Peking University, Beijing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-1090-2844","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xixin","family":"Cao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Peking University, Beijing, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3770855.3818940","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,9]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,8]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 32nd ACM SIGKDD Conference on Knowledge Discovery and Data Mining V.2"]},{"DOI":"10.21203\/rs.3.rs-7718099\/v1","title":["Sentiment analysis of Large Language Models feedback: A multi-model comparative study in programming assessment"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-9404-9814","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Marcin","family":"Jukiewicz","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Adam Mickiewicz University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21203\/rs.3.rs-7718099\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,9,29]]},"abstract":"<title>Abstract<\/title>\n                <p>Despite growing claims about the enhanced capabilities of successive generations of Large Language Models (LLMs), empirical evidence regarding differences in feedback quality and sentiment characteristics remains limited. This study systematically analyzes the sentiment and stylistic features of feedback generated by 18 contemporary LLMs across more than 6,000 student programming assignments. The analyzed models encompassed Anthropic\u2019s claude-3-5-haiku, claude-opus-4-1, and claude-sonnet-4; Deepseek\u2019s deepseek-chat and deepseek-reasoner; Google\u2019s gemini-2.0-flash-lite, gemini-2.0-flash, gemini-2.5-flash-lite, gemini-2.5-flash, and gemini-2.5-pro; and OpenAI\u2019s gpt-4.1-mini, gpt-4.1-nano, gpt-4.1, gpt-4o-mini, gpt-4o, gpt-5-mini, gpt-5-nano, and gpt-5. Using automated sentiment analysis with a RoBERTa-based classifier, the research quantified emotional tone distributions. It examined relationships between sentiment, feedback length, assigned grades, and task characteristics. Results revealed substantial heterogeneity in feedback properties across models: average comment length varied from 42 words (claude-haiku-3.5) to over 270 words (gemini-2.5-flash). At the same time, sentiment distributions differed markedly between providers. Hierarchical clustering identified two distinct groups based on sentiment patterns. However, these did not correspond simply to model architecture or vendor categories. A strong positive correlation (r = 0.707) emerged between feedback sentiment and numerical grades. In contrast, negative feedback demonstrated greater length than positive comments. Inter-model consistency in sentiment evaluation was notably low (ICC = 0.061), indicating substantial variation in how different LLMs express evaluative judgments for identical student responses. These findings demonstrate that automated feedback carries implicit emotional valence that varies systematically across models, highlighting the importance of careful model selection and calibration in educational applications. The study provides quantitative evidence for distinctive feedback characteristics among contemporary LLMs. It underscores the need to consider sentiment dimensions in AI-assisted assessment systems.<\/p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6098366","title":["PRIME: Policy-Reinforced Iterative Multi-agent Execution for Algorithmic Reasoning in Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Jiawei","family":"Xu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Zhenyu","family":"Yu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Ziqian","family":"Bi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Minh Duc","family":"Pham","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Xiaoyi","family":"Qu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Danyang","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6098366","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Large language models have demonstrated remarkable capabilities across diverse reasoning tasks, yet their performance on algorithmic reasoning remains limited. To handle this limitation, we propose PRIME (Policy-Reinforced Iterative Multi-agent Execution), a framework comprising three specialized agents, an executor for step-by-step reasoning, a verifier for constraint checking, and a coordinator for backtracking control, optimized through group relative policy optimization. For comprehensive evaluation, we introduce PRIME-Bench, the largest algorithmic reasoning benchmark to date, comprising 86 tasks across 12 categories with 51,600 instances. Tasks span sorting algorithms, graph and tree structures, automata and state machines, symbolic reasoning, and constraint-based puzzles, with execution traces reaching over one million steps. Compared to baseline approach, PRIME improves average accuracy from 26.8% to 93.8%, a 250% relative gain. The largest improvements occur on tasks requiring sustained state tracking, with Turing machine simulation improving from 9% to 92% and long division from 16% to 94%. Ablation studies identify iterative verification as the primary contributor, preventing the error propagation that causes baseline approaches to fail catastrophically. Analysis across model scales (8B-120B parameters) reveals that smaller models benefit disproportionately, achieving accuracy comparable to models 8\u00d7 larger.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.56726\/irjmets101057","title":["PROMPTOPTIX: A DEFECT-AWARE MULTI-AGENT FRAMEWORK FOR AUTOMATED PROMPT OPTIMIZATION IN LARGE LANGUAGE MODELS"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.56726\/irjmets101057","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,24]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,24]]},"container-title":["International Research Journal of Modernization in Engineering Technology &amp; Science"]},{"DOI":"10.59543\/jidmis.v3.2250","title":["Adaptive Decision Intelligence through Retrieval-Augmented Multi-Agent Large Language Models"],"author":[{"family":"Manish Rana","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.59543\/jidmis.v3.2250","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,21]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The integration of Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) with multi-agent Large Language Models (LLMs) presents transformative potential for adaptive decision intelligence across critical domains. However, significant challenges including evaluation metric standardization, retrieval latency optimization, multi-agent consistency, dynamic knowledge evolution, and human-AI trust management hinder widespread adoption. This research proposes a comprehensive five-layer framework comprising foundation benchmarks, dynamic hybrid retrieval, multi-agent collaboration with weighted consensus, knowledge graph evolution through graph neural networks, and adaptive human-AI interaction. The system employs hybrid sparse-dense retrieval with reinforcement learning optimization, Byzantine fault-tolerant consensus protocols, and temporal knowledge graph embeddings for real-time updates. Experimental validation across healthcare, manufacturing, and cybersecurity domains demonstrates exceptional performance with 92.3% decision accuracy, 412ms retrieval latency, 94.7% agent agreement rate, and 1150 concurrent request capacity, exceeding all established thresholds. The framework effectively bridges the identified gaps, establishing a robust foundation for trustworthy, scalable, and truly adaptive decision intelligence systems with significant practical applicability across diverse domains.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Journal of Intelligent Decision Making and Information Science"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.petsci.2026.05.031","title":["Large language model-based AI agent for well logging data processing and interpretation"],"author":[{"given":"Gang","family":"Luo","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Li-Zhi","family":"Xiao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.petsci.2026.05.031","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,5]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,5]]},"container-title":["Petroleum Science"]},{"DOI":"10.21203\/rs.3.rs-7834717\/v1","title":["Towards Efficient Optimization of Multi-Agent Social Simulation via Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Kun","family":"Zhang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Beihang University"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Xiaoyan","family":"Yu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Beijing Institute of Technology"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Hao","family":"Peng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Beihang University"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Zhe","family":"Yang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Beihang University"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Ye","family":"Tian","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Laboratory for Advanced Computing and Intelligence Engineering"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Hao","family":"Jin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"China Academy of Electronics and Information Technology"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Tuoyu","family":"Feng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"China Academy of Electronics and Information Technology"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21203\/rs.3.rs-7834717\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,27]]},"abstract":"<title>Abstract<\/title>\n                <p>Public opinion on social media during major societal events is highly vulnerable to manipulation bymisinformation and extreme emotions, posing severe threats to mainstream values and public trust.While most existing studies focus on passive detection of negative content, systematic approachesfor proactively optimizing opinion guidance remain scarce.To address this issue, we propose a SocialInfluence Text Generation Network, namely SITGNet, a novel multi-agent social simulation frame-work powered by large language models (LLMs). SITGNet populates a digital social sandbox withcognitively sophisticated agents, each possessing distinct profiles, memories, and behavioral patternsgrounded in empirical data. This enables the high-fidelity reproduction of opinion dynamics, captur-ing emergent phenomena from complex user interactions and psychological transitions. Within thisplatform, we introduce Guiding Agents, a specialized class of agents designed to proactively steerdiscourse. These agents leverage a multi-stage retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) pipeline to syn-thesize information from external knowledge bases, grounding their outputs in factual evidence togenerate nuanced and influential content. Through extensive, large-scale simulations, we systemati-cally evaluate various intervention strategies. Our results demonstrate that a hybrid deployment ofGuiding Agents can effectively suppress negative sentiment propagation, alleviate group polariza-tion, and guide opinion trajectories toward rational outcomes. In addition to building the simulationframework, this study treats Social Influence Text Generation as a key modeling objective, enablingsystematic evaluation of text-based opinion guidance strategies.<\/p>"},{"DOI":"10.69997\/pse.121793","title":["Sketch2Simulation: Automating Flowsheet Generation Via Multi-Agent Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Emma","family":"Pajak","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Imperial College London, The Sargent Centre for Process Systems Engineering, Department of Chemical Engineering"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.69997\/pse.121793","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,26]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Converting process flow diagrams into complete simulation models remains a persistent bottleneck in process systems engineering (PSE), requiring significant manual effort and simulator-specific expertise. Although advances in diagram interpretation and automated model generation have been made, these tasks are typically addressed in isolation, limiting the automation of end-to-end workflows. This work introduces Sketch2Simulation, a unified computational framework that automates flowsheet generation directly from raw engineering diagrams using a multi-agent large language model (LLM) architecture. The proposed framework integrates three coordinated layers: (i) Diagram Parsing and Interpretation, (ii) Simulation Model Synthesis, and (iii) Multi-level Validation. In the first layer, multimodal LLM agents extract process semantics, identify unit operations and stream connectivity, and resolve implicit structural features. This information is encoded into a directed graph-based intermediate representation that captures process topology while enforcing simulator-compatible constraints. This intermediate representation serves as a formal interface between diagram interpretation and simulator execution, enabling consistent translation of unstructured visual inputs into simulator-compatible models. The second layer translates this representation into a simulation model through sequential agents responsible for thermodynamic specification, object instantiation, and operating condition assignment, culminating in simulation execution within Aspen HYSYS. The use of a multi-agent architecture enables decomposition of the workflow into specialised reasoning tasks spanning multimodal interpretation, structured model synthesis, and simulator interaction, improving scalability, interpretability, and robustness compared to monolithic LLM approaches. The final layer introduces validation at multiple stages, including schema enforcement and an execution-and-correction loop that iteratively resolves runtime errors to ensure model validity. The framework is evaluated across four case studies of increasing complexity, including industrial-scale flowsheets with recycle loops. Results demonstrate consistent generation of simulation models with high structural fidelity, achieving near-complete recovery of process topology (e.g., connection consistency \u2265 0.93, stream consistency \u2265 0.96). Performance degradation is primarily associated with increased diagram complexity and dense interconnections. This work demonstrates that diagram-to-simulation transformation can be formulated as a unified computational problem, reducing reliance on manual model construction and advancing the digitalisation of PSE workflows. Crucially, this enables faster iteration between conceptual design and simulation, lowering the barrier to deploying high-fidelity models in both research and industrial settings.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Proceedings of the 3rd Foundations of Process\/Product Analytics and Machine Learning (FOPAM 2026)"]},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,1,1]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>As larger large language models (LLMs) demonstrate increasing proficiency in strategic reasoning tasks, their computational demands limit widespread deployment and the creation of agentic swarms. This study explores whether fine-tuning can yield smaller, more efficient models that achieve high performance in game-theoretic decision-making. We queried a large pretrained model on 20 diverse scenarios that associate canonical social dilemmas from game theory with social contexts, recording both the answers and the model\u2019s reasoning. Responses obtained this way were then used to fine-tune a smaller model from the same family. Our results indicate that fine-tuning significantly improved the smaller model\u2019s alignment with the larger model, achieving a mean overall increase of 46% (median: 32%) in behavioral similarity. Notably, improvements extended beyond training examples, with an 18% mean performance gain (median: 14%) in novel social contexts. When presented with re-parametrized versions of games from its training set, the fine-tuned model consistently reproduced its original strategies, indicating strong generalization to games with identical structures but altered payoffs. These findings suggest that fine-tuning enables smaller models to internalize aspects of strategic reasoning, enhancing their capacity for multi-agent interfacing while maintaining efficiency. This approach offers a promising pathway for developing cost-effective, specialized models for strategic decision-making, advancing both AI scalability and the study of computational social cognition.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1115\/1.4072118","title":["Transferring Strategic Thinking From Large to Small Language Models for Multi-Agent Systems"],"author":[{"given":"Nunzio","family":"Lore","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"id":[{"id":"https:\/\/ror.org\/04t5xt781","id-type":"ROR","asserted-by":"publisher"}],"name":"Northeastern University Network Science Institute, , 177 Huntington Avenue, , \u00a0","place":["Boston, MA, 02115"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Sepehr","family":"Ilami","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"id":[{"id":"https:\/\/ror.org\/04t5xt781","id-type":"ROR","asserted-by":"publisher"}],"name":"Northeastern University Department of Mechanical and Industrial Engineering, , 360 Huntington Avenue, , \u00a0","place":["Boston, MA, 02115"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Babak","family":"Heydari","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"id":[{"id":"https:\/\/ror.org\/04t5xt781","id-type":"ROR","asserted-by":"publisher"}],"name":"Northeastern University Network Science Institute and Department of Mechanical and Industrial Engineering, , 360 Huntington Avenue, , \u00a0","place":["Boston, MA, 02115"]}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,8]]},"container-title":["ASME Open Journal of Engineering"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,1,1]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1115\/1.4072118"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6293248","title":["A large language model-guided machinery fault diagnosis model based on multi-task prompt tuning and adaptive adversarial distillation"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0006-0078-2315","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Ziyue","family":"Jiang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-3204-2486","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Jianbo","family":"Yu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6293248","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The large language model (LLM) has achieved significant success in artificial intelligence, which demonstrates its powerful ability and the potential for application in other fields. It is feasible to train or fine-tune an LLM for machinery fault diagnosis, which offers new possibilities for improving the generalization of models. However, there is a significant requirement for computing power and data for training and fine-tuning LLM. Thus, it is challenging to propose a general industrial large model for machinery fault diagnosis, which has efficient processing capability in big sensor data. In this study, an LLM-guided machinery fault diagnosis model (LGMFD) is proposed for fault diagnosis of multiple machines. Firstly, a prompt-tuning module is proposed in LGMFD, which is used to enable the pre-trained LLM to learn fault diagnosis knowledge. Then, a multitask learning module is constructed to identify the faulty machines and their fault types, which enables the model to extract knowledge from the data of different machines. Secondly, an adaptive adversarial distillation module is proposed to improve the feature learning performance of the student model, which guides deep neural networks (DNNs) for fault diagnosis by the fine-tuned LLM. Finally, the experimental results on six datasets show that LGMFD has a good performance on feature extraction and machinery fault diagnosis.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6433709","title":["Synergizing Transformers and Residual Kriging for Nationwide Spatiotemporal Haze Early Warning with\u00a0Large Language Model-based PM Agent"],"author":[{"given":"Pu  Yun","family":"Kow","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Pu  Ern","family":"Kow","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6433709","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>In recent years, sustainability has emerged as a critical priority in response to escalating environmental degradation, making air pollution control a key challenge. In the context of data-driven urban management, this study proposes Spatiotemporal Aerosol Representation with Transformer (StAR-Former), a hybrid Environmental Intelligence framework designed to address the complexities of spatiotemporal PM2.5\u00a0forecasting. Distinct from traditional approaches, StAR-Former decouples temporal modeling from spatial interpolation: it utilizes a lightweight Transformer-based architecture for accurate point forecasting and integrates residual Kriging to construct spatially continuous pollution maps. Using hourly observations from 2020 to 2024, experimental results demonstrate that StAR-Former consistently outperforms benchmark LSTM models and foundation models (i.e. Chronos and Lag-Llama) across all horizons. The model exhibits superior accuracy and generalization, particularly in capturing long-term dependencies, while its best computational efficiency required for sustainable smart city deployment. Regarding spatial analysis, the integrated residual Kriging module significantly enhances regional estimation reliability, yielding a lower Mean Absolute Error (MAE) compared to ordinary Kriging. The framework&amp;apos;s robustness is further validated under extreme conditions, including industrial emission zones and river-dust events. Finally, to bridge the gap between technical monitoring and public action, a PM Agent driven by a Large Language Model (LLM) is developed. This module translates forecasts into concise, actionable health guidance, providing a scalable and energy-efficient solution for resilient urban air quality management.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1037\/neu0001079.supp","title":["Supplemental Material for A Large Language Model-Based Self-Learning and Critical Agent Framework for Multimodal Alzheimer\u2019s Disease Diagnosis"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1037\/neu0001079.supp","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,19]]},"container-title":["Neuropsychology"]},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,12]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>This preliminary study introduces and evaluates a router-based multi-agent framework for automated foundation design calculations through intelligent task classification and expert selection. Three configurations were assessed: single-agent processing, multi-agent designer-checker architecture, and router-based expert selection, using baseline models including DeepSeek R1, ChatGPT 4 Turbo, Grok 3, and Gemini 2.5 Pro. Initial evaluation on 27 test cases with triple-trial execution shows promising performance: the router-based system achieved 95.00% for shallow foundations and 90.63% for pile design, representing improvements of 8.75 and 3.13 percentage points over standalone Grok 3, respectively, and outperforming conventional workflows by 10.0\u201343.75 percentage points. Grok 3 demonstrated superior standalone performance, indicating enhanced large language model (LLM) mathematical reasoning capabilities. The dual-tier classification framework successfully distinguished foundation types, enabling appropriate analytical approaches. While these preliminary results suggest router-based multi-agent systems as a promising approach for foundation design automation, the limited sample size necessitates comprehensive validation on larger, more diverse datasets before deployment recommendations. Safety\u2013critical requirements necessitate continued human oversight in professional applications. This work provides a methodological foundation for future research in AI-assisted geotechnical engineering.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1007\/s43503-026-00088-8","title":["Large language model-based multi-agent systems for automated foundation design: router-driven task classification and expert selection framework"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-9878-8504","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Sompote","family":"Youwai","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"David","family":"Phim","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Vianne Gayl","family":"Murcia","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Rianne Clair","family":"Onas","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,2]]},"container-title":["AI in Civil Engineering"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,2]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s43503-026-00088-8"},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3820664.3820708","title":["A Multi-Agent Collaborative Chest X-Ray Diagnostic System Integrating DenseNet, Knowledge Graph, and Large Language Model"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-8355-1470","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xian-Fang","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer Science and Technology, Henan Institute of Technology, Xinxiang, Henan, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-5803-1059","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Kai-Long","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer Science and Technology, Henan Institute of Technology, Xinxiang, Henan, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-8964-1414","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Shu-Mei","family":"Hou","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer Science and Technology, Henan Institute of Technology, Xinxiang, Henan, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-5051-9963","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zhao-Hui","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Computer Science and Technology, Henan Institute of Technology, Xinxiang, Henan, China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3820664.3820708","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,17]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,8,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,17]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 2026 2nd International Symposium on Bioinformatics and Computational Biology"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6253390","title":["Market-Consistent and Secure Demand Response in Storage-Rich Integrated Energy Systems Under Uncertainty and Hybrid Cyberattacks: A Large Language Model-Guided Multi-Agent Deep Reinforcement Learning Approach"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-7911-581X","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Reza","family":"Sepehrzad","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Nima","family":"Khosravi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-2476-3165","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Milad","family":"Moafi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-0270-2944","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"HOSSEIN","family":"HOSSEINALIBEIKI","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-6629-5134","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Ahmed","family":"Al durra","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4609-9152","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Mahdieh S.","family":"Sadabadi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6253390","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Energy storage systems are a cornerstone of deployable demand response (DR) in integrated energy systems (IESs) because it buffers renewable intermittency, mitigates market volatility, and provides a cyber-resilient flexibility reserve when sensing or communications are compromised. This paper proposes a storage-centric, safe, and uncertainty-aware DR architecture that coordinates battery energy storage systems and thermal energy storage alongside flexible electric and heat loads, and renewable energy sources. The architecture couples a market-settlement layer with real-time operation through a large language model (LLM)-guided hierarchical multi-agent deep reinforcement learning (MADRL) controller. Cyber- and uncertainty-aware state representations, adversarial and domain-randomized training, and a conditional value-at-risk (CVaR)-based risk term are used to harden policies against forecast errors, price shocks, and hybrid cyberattacks (e.g., false data injection (FDI) and denial-of-service (DoS), hybrid attacks, and delay). A constrained action repair and projection layer guarantees device and network feasibility during execution, ensuring that storage dispatch and DR modulation remain within voltage, line-loading, comfort, and charge-discharge limits. Evaluations on three systems (IEEE 69-bus, Caracas 141-bus, and IEEE 342-node) show that storage absorbs a growing share of flexibility under stress, increasing from 37\u201344% (nominal) to 45\u201352% (combined uncertainty plus attacks), which stabilizes service delivery and reduces violations. Under combined stress, the proposed method sustains 93.8\u201396.4% DR delivery with 1.2\u20132.3% violation rates while preserving robust profitability and improved tail outcomes relative to advanced learning baselines. The results position storage-backed, safe MADRL as a scalable pathway for market-consistent and cyber-resilient DR in multi-energy distribution systems.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6756498","title":["Industrial Multi-AI Agent\u00a0Collaborative\u00a0System based on Large-Small Model Fusion and Retrieval-Augmented Generation"],"author":[{"given":"Xiao","family":"Lai","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Han","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-8902-5460","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Min","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Li","family":"Bai","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6756498","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>With the rapid advancement of intelligent manufacturing, industrial systems generate vast amounts of unstructured data, yet face significant challenges in data utilization, knowledge retrieval accuracy, decision-making efficiency, and human-machine collaboration. To address these issues, this paper proposes a large-small model fusion and retrieval-augmented generation based industrial multi-AI agent collaborative system (IMACS). First, a RAG-driven data governance pipeline is introduced, featuring IndDocMaster (a high-precision unstructured data parsing model) enabling end-to-end document understanding through multi-source fusion and a data flywheel mechanism, significantly enhancing knowledge extraction quality. Second, a hierarchical agent architecture from L1 to L4 is designed, achieving cognitive-execution synergy via large-model orchestration and small-model execution. Finally, an Agent-Human-Cyber-Physical System (A-HCPS) framework is established to enable autonomous, collaborative decision-making. Experimental results show that the proposed system outperforms existing methods in industrial data parsing, knowledge retrieval, and near-expert-level maintenance decision-making with over 60% faster response, demonstrating its effectiveness in advancing industrial intelligence.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6219015","title":["Synthesis large language models and machine learning agent"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0003-3642-9538","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Roman","family":"Zikov","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6219015","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The capabilities of large language models (LLMs) continue to grow daily. Their potential applications in the gaming industry are vast and constantly expanding. This article presents a concept for synthesizing a large language model using the Unity game engine&amp;apos;s toolset for creating intelligent agents. It begins with an overview of the current, specific use of these tools in game development, describing their practical applications and conceptual plans. This is particularly relevant for dialogue management and adapting behavior in response to player actions and choices, potentially supported by speech interfaces or dynamic skill tree systems driven by AI agent logic. A model of a hypothetical non-player character (NPC) will be described, using LLMs to communicate with the player and internal algorithms for interacting with an intelligent agent as feedback. The model&amp;apos;s applicability to games will be assessed, a potential architecture will be discussed, and a direction for further research in this area will be outlined.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.20944\/preprints202606.1111.v1","title":["Dynamic Task-Chain Reconfiguration for Cooperative Counter-UAV Defense: A Multi-Agent Large Language Model Framework for Automated Heuristic Design"],"author":[{"given":"Yihao","family":"Zhong","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-0730-6830","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Changsheng","family":"Yin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ruopeng","family":"Yang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yuantao","family":"Yang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yiwei","family":"Lu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yongqi","family":"Wen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yongqi","family":"Shi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-6229-9587","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Bo","family":"Huang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yu","family":"Tao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jinyin","family":"Bai","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.20944\/preprints202606.1111.v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,17]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The growing affordability, autonomy, and swarming of small unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs) turn low-altitude defense from single-shot interception into a multi-node cooperative decision problem, in which the loss of sensing, coordination, or engagement nodes breaks the closed loops linking them. This study formulates their recovery as the dynamic reconfiguration of cooperative counter-UAV task chains. Given a pre-disturbance plan and a set of failed defending nodes, reconfiguration is modeled as a constrained bi-objective optimization balancing recovered engagement effectiveness against the change to the baseline plan, and is solved by Multi-Agent Heuristic Evolution (MAHE), an automated heuristic design framework whose evolution, coordinator, repair, and reflection agents\u2014driven by a large language model\u2014evolve scoring heuristics for a fixed reconfiguration solver. Across instances of varying scale and under light-to-heavy node loss, MAHE outperforms both a single-agent heuristic-design counterpart and a range of hand-crafted solvers: the latter lose most of their solution quality as the problem grows, whereas MAHE preserves it and sustains high recovery at a nearly constant reconfiguration cost; an ablation confirms that its agents contribute complementary gains. These results indicate that automatically generated, reconfiguration-specific heuristics provide a scalable route to dynamic, heterogeneous, and constraint-intensive counter-UAV task planning.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.3390\/app152111619","title":["Multi-Agent Multimodal Large Language Model Framework for Automated Interpretation of Fuel Efficiency Analytics in Public Transportation"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4049-539X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zhipeng","family":"Ma","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"SDU Center for Energy Informatics, The Maersk Mc-Kinney Moller Institute, Faculty of Engineering, University of Southern Denmark, DK-5230 Odense, Denmark"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ali Rida","family":"Bahja","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Institute for Technologies and Management of Digital Transformation, University of Wuppertal, D-42119 Wuppertal, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Andreas","family":"Burgdorf","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Institute for Technologies and Management of Digital Transformation, University of Wuppertal, D-42119 Wuppertal, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-0111-1813","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Andr\u00e9","family":"Pomp","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Institute for Technologies and Management of Digital Transformation, University of Wuppertal, D-42119 Wuppertal, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-1969-559X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Tobias","family":"Meisen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Institute for Technologies and Management of Digital Transformation, University of Wuppertal, D-42119 Wuppertal, Germany"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-5678-6602","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Bo N\u00f8rregaard","family":"J\u00f8rgensen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"SDU Center for Energy Informatics, The Maersk Mc-Kinney Moller Institute, Faculty of Engineering, University of Southern Denmark, DK-5230 Odense, Denmark"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9134-1032","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zheng Grace","family":"Ma","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"SDU Center for Energy Informatics, The Maersk Mc-Kinney Moller Institute, Faculty of Engineering, University of Southern Denmark, DK-5230 Odense, Denmark"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3390\/app152111619","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,30]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Enhancing fuel efficiency in public transportation requires the integration of complex multimodal data into interpretable, decision-relevant insights. However, traditional analytics and visualization methods often yield fragmented outputs that demand extensive human interpretation, limiting scalability and consistency. This study presents a multi-agent framework that leverages multimodal large language models (LLMs) to automate data narration and energy insight generation. The framework coordinates three specialized agents, including a data narration agent, an LLM-as-a-judge agent, and an optional human-in-the-loop evaluator, to iteratively transform analytical artifacts into coherent, stakeholder-oriented reports. The system is validated through a real-world case study on public bus transportation in Northern Jutland, Denmark, where fuel efficiency data from 4006 trips are analyzed using Gaussian Mixture Model clustering. Comparative experiments across five state-of-the-art LLMs and three prompting paradigms identify GPT-4.1 mini with Chain-of-Thought prompting as the optimal configuration, achieving 97.3% narrative accuracy while balancing interpretability and computational cost. The findings demonstrate that multi-agent orchestration significantly enhances factual precision, coherence, and scalability in LLM-based reporting. The proposed framework establishes a replicable and domain-adaptive methodology for AI-driven narrative generation and decision support in energy informatics.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Applied Sciences"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.chb.2025.108730","title":["Impact of mindset types and social community compositions on opinion dynamics: A large language model-based multi-agent simulation study"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2043-8320","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Guozhu","family":"Ding","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zuer","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Shan","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-6507-9613","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jie","family":"Cao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zhuohai","family":"Ye","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.chb.2025.108730","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,11]]},"container-title":["Computers in Human Behavior"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.isci.2025.113867","title":["Automated building energy modeling for energy retrofits using a large language model-based multi-agent framework"],"author":[{"given":"Jie","family":"Lu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zeyu","family":"Zheng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Max","family":"Langtry","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Monty","family":"Jackson","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yang","family":"Zhao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Chenxin","family":"Feng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ruqian","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Chaobo","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jian","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ruchi","family":"Choudhary","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.isci.2025.113867","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,11]]},"container-title":["iScience"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/ceepe69795.2026.11552199","title":["Multi-Agent Large Language Model Framework for Power Grid Emergency Material Support: Construction and Application"],"author":[{"given":"Zhenqi","family":"Yang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"State Grid Jibei Electric Power Co., Ltd. Material Branch,State Grid Jibei Electric Power Co., Ltd. Material Branch,Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Lu","family":"Cui","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"State Grid Jibei Electric Power Co., Ltd. Material Branch,State Grid Jibei Electric Power Co., Ltd. Material Branch,Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ling","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"State Grid Jibei Electric Power Co., Ltd. Material Branch,State Grid Jibei Electric Power Co., Ltd. Material Branch,Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xiaoying","family":"Zhu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"State Grid Jibei Electric Power Co., Ltd. Material Branch,State Grid Jibei Electric Power Co., Ltd. Material Branch,Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xiaohan","family":"Qi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"State Grid Jibei Electric Power Co., Ltd. Material Branch,State Grid Jibei Electric Power Co., Ltd. Material Branch,Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Liu","family":"Yang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"State Grid Jibei Electric Power Co., Ltd. Material Branch,State Grid Jibei Electric Power Co., Ltd. Material Branch,Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zhipu","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"North China Electric Power University,School of Energy Power and Mechanical Engineering,Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Heng","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"North China Electric Power University,School of Energy Power and Mechanical Engineering,Beijing,China"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/ceepe69795.2026.11552199","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,17]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,17]]},"container-title":["2026 9th International Conference on Energy, Electrical and Power Engineering (CEEPE)"]},{"DOI":"10.2196\/preprints.108214","title":["Nodule-AFM: An Intelligent Multi-Agent Large Language Model System for Personalized Active Follow-up Management of Patients with Pulmonary Nodules: Development and Feasibility Evaluation (Preprint)"],"author":[{"given":"Dahai","family":"Liu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Chaofeng","family":"Yang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Wen","family":"Deng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Meiling","family":"Gu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Wenxiao","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yibin","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Zhenyu","family":"Ren","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xing","family":"Lv","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2196\/preprints.108214","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,30]]},"abstract":"<sec>\n                  <title>BACKGROUND<\/title>\n                  <p>Traditional pulmonary nodule follow-up management relies heavily on manual workflows, leading to inefficiencies and inconsistencies that hinder early lung cancer detection.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                <sec>\n                  <title>OBJECTIVE<\/title>\n                  <p>This study aimed to develop and validate Nodule-AFM, an intelligent, automated system designed to enhance both the efficiency and personalization of pulmonary nodule follow-up management.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                <sec>\n                  <title>METHODS<\/title>\n                  <p>We developed Nodule-AFM, an AI-driven active follow-up management system based on a multi-agent collaborative framework comprising an Information Agent (IA) for patient data retrieval and synthesis, an Active Dialogue Agent (ADA) for adaptive goal-oriented conversations using Chain-of-Thought Self-Consistency (CoT-SC), and a Summary and Validation Agent (SVA) for quality control and structured reporting. The system was developed using 2,460 follow-up recordings for knowledge construction and inquiry categorization. Prospective evaluation utilized 283 real-world follow-up dialogues, with 85 recordings allocated to validation for prompt engineering and 198 recordings to independent testing. Four experienced physicians independently assessed the 198 test cases.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                <sec>\n                  <title>RESULTS<\/title>\n                  <p>Nodule-AFM achieved a 94% conversation completion rate, 84% accuracy in dialogue information extraction, and 79% accuracy in summarizing patient intentions. The system maintained high output quality, with LLM-based evaluation scores exceeding 4.9 across multiple safety and relevance dimensions. Furthermore, it demonstrated generalizability across different LLM backbones and outperformed stand-alone Chain-of-Thought or Self-Consistency methods.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>\n                <sec>\n                  <title>CONCLUSIONS<\/title>\n                  <p>The findings indicate that a collaborative multi-agent framework can support scalable, clinically reliable, and patient-specific follow-up communication. Nodule-AFM represents a novel LLM-driven approach to pulmonary nodule follow-up management that improves efficiency, accuracy, and patient engagement while reducing clinician workload.<\/p>\n                <\/sec>"},{"DOI":"10.32604\/cmc.2026.083337","title":["A Grounded Multi-Agent Multimodal Large Language Model Framework for Interpretable Risk Assessment in Driving Scenes"],"author":[{"given":"Chien-Hao","family":"Tseng","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Min-Yu","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Meng-Wei","family":"Lin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jyh-Horng","family":"Wu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Chung-I","family":"Huang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.32604\/cmc.2026.083337","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Computers, Materials &amp; Continua"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.aei.2025.104294","title":["Large language model-empowered dynamic scheduling for intelligent hybrid flow shop using multi-agent deep reinforcement learning"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-5918-8800","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Wenbin","family":"Gu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yushang","family":"Cao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yuxin","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Nuandong","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Lei","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Na","family":"Tang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Minghai","family":"Yuan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Fengque","family":"Pei","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.aei.2025.104294","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,4]]},"container-title":["Advanced Engineering Informatics"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7204118","title":["Xyraiq: A Multi-Tier Behavioral Stress Benchmark for Large Language Model Evaluation with Hashable Audit Provenance"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-1175-7749","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Roshan George","family":"Thomas","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7204118","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>This working paper was conceived, designed, and authored by the named human researcher based on direct observational experience with LLM deployment in operational GCC environments. The core research problem, architectural design decisions, the Stress Signature mechanism, and the Persona Pack taxonomy originate entirely with the author. AI-assisted tools were used for drafting support in Sections 2 and 5, literature crossreferencing, and document formatting only. Sections 1, 3, and 6 reflect the author's direct research observations and are substantially the author's own writing. This disclosure is made in full accordance with SSRN's AI disclosure requirements (updated June 2026).<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6461164","title":["Knowledge-Graph Enhanced Multi-modal Large Language Model For Domain QA"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-3608-1810","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Tianlong","family":"Guo","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Derong","family":"Shen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-5307-4893","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Yue","family":"Kou","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0166-1324","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Tiezheng","family":"Nie","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6461164","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Multi-modal Large-Language Models (MLLMs) extend LLMs to multi-modal data to fit more applications.However, these MLLMs inherently face several limitations, including poor generalization ability, insufficient knowledge retention, inadequate multi-modal capabilities, and limited reasoning abilities.To address these challenges, this paper proposes a novel framework: Knowledge-Graph Enhanced Multi-modal Language Model (KGE-MLLM), specifically designed for domain-specific multi-modal Question Answering (QA). KGE-MLLM integrates a multi-modal knowledge graph (KG) as additional modality alongside a KG-RAG mechanism to effectively augment the model with external knowledge. Furthermore, a multi-modal fusion module is proposed to fuse multi-modal data include image, text and knowledge graph. Besides, it employs a specially fine-tuned reasoning module to achieve robust multi-modal fusion and facilitate complex multi-hop reasoning. Ultimately, a dedicated answer generation model synthesizes the multi-modal reasoning insights and retrieved external knowledge to provide comprehensive answers. The effectiveness of the proposed KGE-MLLM framework is rigorously validated through comparative experiments against baseline models of similar scale and comprehensive ablation studies, confirming the significant contributions of each component.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3786181.3788717","title":["MAsFL: Data-Secure, Efficient and Accurate Fault Localization with Multi-Agent Small Language Models"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-0758-8482","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Duong","family":"Pham Duc","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"National Defense Academy, Yokosuka, Kanagawa, Japan"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-5699-1964","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Hiroshi","family":"Sato","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"National Defense Academy, Yokosuka, Kanagawa, Japan"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-7733-2376","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Masao","family":"Kubo","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"National Defense Academy, Yokosuka, Kanagawa, Japan"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3786181.3788717","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,12]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,12]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 3rd International Workshop on Large Language Models For Code"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.neucom.2026.133642","title":["Efficient multi-agent policy learning using knowledge distillation of large language models"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-8053-7859","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Liqiang","family":"Tian","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-1228-2757","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Peiliang","family":"Wu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-2867-7512","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Qian","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0008-6547-5150","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Bingyi","family":"Mao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-7683-2776","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Wenbai","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.neucom.2026.133642","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,7]]},"container-title":["Neurocomputing"]},{"DOI":"10.21203\/rs.3.rs-8785621\/v1","title":["Epistemic Field Theory: Predicting Hallucination in Large Language Models via Multi-Model Consensus"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-2760-6073","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"AZRIL BIN","family":"HAMZAH","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"eptim.ai"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6188-9470","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"SHASHA","family":"TENG","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"eptim.ai"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21203\/rs.3.rs-8785621\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,5]]},"abstract":"<title>Abstract<\/title>\n                <p>Large language models exhibit hallucination\u2014generating confident but incorrect outputs\u2014at rates that undermine their reliability in high-stakes applications. We introduce Epistemic Field Theory (EFT), a formal framework that predicts hallucination probability from multi-model consensus. EFT defines a consensus field \u03c3 \u2208 [0, 1] over query space and derives the hallucination predictor P(H) = (1 \u2212 \u03c3) \u00b7 \u03b7, where \u03b7 is a model-specific noise coefficient. We establish theoretical conditions under which consensus bounds error probability and prove that independent model errors yield superlinear consensus-reliability scaling. Empirical validation across 13,728 responses from four models in three domains confirms the core prediction: consensus and hallucination correlate at r = \u22120.38 (p &lt; 0.001), with hallucination rates dropping from 51.9% (\u03c3 &lt; 0.2) to 5.9% (\u03c3 = 1.0). The framework provides a principled, model-agnostic mechanism for uncertainty-aware decision gating in automated pipelines.<\/p>"},{"DOI":"10.21203\/rs.3.rs-9575030\/v1","title":["Multi-Agent Debate System Based on Large Language Models: Structured Deliberation and Validation in Satellite Communications"],"author":[{"given":"Susana","family":"G\u00f3mez","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Malaga"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Alejandro","family":"Mozo","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Malaga"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Tom\u00e1s","family":"Navarro","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"European Space Agency"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Sergio","family":"G\u00e1lvez","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Malaga"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Francisco L.","family":"Valverde","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Malaga"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21203\/rs.3.rs-9575030\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,11]]},"abstract":"<title>Abstract<\/title>\n                <p>Structured multi-agent debates among Large Language Models (LLMs) have emerged as a powerful paradigm for enhancing reasoning reliability and argumentative coherence. Motivated by the European Space Agency's (ESA) interest in trustworthy AI for space operations, this study proposes a moderated, domain-adaptive multi-agent debate framework applied to the high-stakes domain of satellite communications (SatCom). Specifically, it assesses (i) the efficacy of structured deliberation against single-agent baselines, and (ii) the impact of model heterogeneity versus homogeneity. A single-agent baseline is compared against a multi-agent framework deploying a moderator and two domain-specialized experts. These systems utilize local 70B-parameter LLMs in homogeneous (Llama-3.3) and heterogeneous (Llama-3.3 + DeepSeek-R1 + Qwen-2.5) configurations, all augmented with a shared, curated Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) corpus combining academic institutional sources and ESA material from the Nebula portal (SatNex V programme). Outputs from 213 technical queries are evaluated via LLM-as-a-judge across three phases: baseline proficiency, strategic reasoning, and executive readiness. Single-agent systems lead in encyclopedic tasks, where retrieval suffices over deliberation. However, both multi-agent configurations outperform in strategic reasoning, with heterogeneous debates achieving superior performance in executive scenarios by victory margins of up to 2.75 points on a 10-point scale. These results validate architectural diversity as a decisive factor in resolving high-complexity technical conflicts. Ultimately, this work delivers a generalizable, fully traceable deliberation framework suitable for real-world, mission-critical environments. Code, prompts, and evaluation data are publicly available at https:\/\/github.com\/amozo-es\/multi-agent-debate\/.<\/p>"},{"DOI":"10.54254\/2755-2721\/2026.ch34641","title":["A Survey of Large Language Models in Multi-Agent Systems: Advancing Coordination and Intelligence"],"author":[{"given":"Zhongzhi","family":"Gong","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Wenzhou University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.54254\/2755-2721\/2026.ch34641","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,23]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Multi-agent systems(MASs) are more and more significant for solving complex real problems that demanded coordinated decision and interaction in dynamic environments. However, traditional ways, especially in reinforcement learning(RL), are not competent in the situation, including language grounding, multimodal perception, long-horizon planning, and cross-domain generalization. In contrast, large language models (LLMs) show a stronger and promising potential by equipping agents with strong reasoning, planning, and communication capabilities in its distinctive paradigm shift. In this survey,we will comprehensively demonstrate emerging LLM-based multi-agent systems in four key aspects:(1) communication and interaction, which governs how agents swaps information with coordinated reasoning;(2) system architecture and coordination, defining the way to assign and organise complex tasks;(3) evaluation and benchmarking, which is used to measure agents\u2019abilities in different environments;(4) learning, evolution, characterizing how agents improve, adapt and generalize. Through these advanced procedure of refereed dimensions, we reveal how LLMs reshape MASs\u2019 designing space, which can realize the more flexible, extensible and human-aligned coordination strategies. Finally, we discuss open challenges including efficiency, reliability and standardized evaluation, and outline future research directions toward robust and autonomous multi-agent intelligence.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Applied and Computational Engineering"]},{"DOI":"10.21203\/rs.3.rs-9045948\/v1","title":["Neurosymbolic Multi-Agent Artificial Intelligence versus General-Purpose Large Language Models for Clinical Decision Support in Ileus and Volvulus"],"author":[{"given":"mete","family":"ucdal","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Etimesgut Asker Hastanesi"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Sefa","family":"Keskin","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Beytepe Asker Hastanesi"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"karya","family":"yurtsever","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Hacettepe University Hospital"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Leyla","family":"Eybatova","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Ba\u015fkent University Hospital"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21203\/rs.3.rs-9045948\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,10]]},"abstract":"<title>Abstract<\/title>\n                <p>\n                  <bold>Background<\/bold>\n                  General-purpose large language models (LLMs) demonstrate variable diagnostic accuracy and residual hallucination when applied to complex surgical emergencies. Whether a neurosymbolic multi-agent architecture\u2014integrating domain-specific vision-language models, medically fine-tuned reasoning engines, and compositional verification agents\u2014can outperform monolithic LLMs in ileus and volvulus case assessment remains unexplored.\n                  <bold>Methods<\/bold>\n                  We conducted a retrospective diagnostic accuracy study using 133 adult case vignettes (median age 62 years; 57.9% male) reconstructed from PubMed-indexed case reports published between January 2022 and December 2025. Three AI systems were evaluated: ChatGPT (GPT-4 Turbo), Gemini 2.0 Pro, and a sequential neurosymbolic multi-agent hybrid system comprising a radiology vision-language agent (Hulu-Med 32B), a clinical reasoning agent (Med-PaLM 2), and a compositional validation agent (Gyan LLM). Standardized prompts were submitted in zero-shot configuration. Two blinded expert assessors independently evaluated five predefined criteria: diagnostic accuracy, treatment appropriateness, hallucination presence, explanation adequacy, and critical safety errors. Inter-rater reliability was assessed using Cohen\u2019s kappa. McNemar\u2019s test with Bonferroni correction was used for pairwise comparisons.\n                  <bold>Results<\/bold>\n                  The neurosymbolic multi-agent system achieved significantly higher diagnostic accuracy (75.2%; 95% CI: 66.9\u201382.2%) compared with ChatGPT (60.2%; 95% CI: 51.4\u201368.5%; p\u2009&lt;\u20090.001) and Gemini (58.6%; 95% CI: 49.8\u201367.0%; p\u2009&lt;\u20090.001). The multi-agent system also demonstrated superior treatment appropriateness (74.4% vs. 63.9% and 61.7%; both p\u2009&lt;\u20090.017), markedly lower hallucination rates (1.5% vs. 15.0% and 9.8%; both p\u2009&lt;\u20090.001), and zero critical safety errors (0% vs. 3.8% and 2.3%). Subgroup analysis revealed perfect diagnostic accuracy (100%) for volvulus cases in the multi-agent system versus 78.6% and 75.0% for the single-model systems. Performance convergence was observed in diagnostically ambiguous entities including Ogilvie syndrome (67.6% vs. 48.6% and 51.4%) and toxic megacolon (50.0% vs. 41.7%).\n                  <bold>Conclusions<\/bold>\n                  A neurosymbolic multi-agent pipeline that decomposes the clinical reasoning workflow into specialized perception, synthesis, and verification stages significantly outperforms general-purpose LLMs in diagnosing and managing ileus-spectrum and volvulus-spectrum emergencies. The architectural separation of neural pattern recognition from symbolic rule-based verification substantially reduces hallucination and eliminates critical safety errors. These findings support the integration of neurosymbolic design principles in clinical AI systems for acute abdominal pathology, while underscoring persistent limitations in diagnostically ambiguous conditions.\n                <\/p>"},{"DOI":"10.1109\/iccit68739.2025.11491743","title":["Calibrating Stereotype Preference with Multi-Agent Graph Reasoning in Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Md Nur","family":"Amin","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Institute for Intelligent Cyber-Physical Systems (ICPS), Heilbronn University,K&#x00FC;nzelsau,Germany"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Alexander","family":"Jesser","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Institute for Intelligent Cyber-Physical Systems (ICPS), Heilbronn University,K&#x00FC;nzelsau,Germany"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/iccit68739.2025.11491743","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,19]]},"container-title":["2025 28th International Conference on Computer and Information Technology (ICCIT)"]},{"DOI":"10.26599\/tst.2026.9010062","title":["Agent Network for Open Multi-Agent Collaboration with Shared Cognition"],"author":[{"given":"Lan","family":"Zhang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yunhao","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.26599\/tst.2026.9010062","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6]]},"abstract":"<jats:sec>\n                    <jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                    <jats:p>The history of information infrastructure can be read as a history of connectivity. Transmission Control Protocol\/Internet Protocol (TCP\/IP) made heterogeneous hosts reachable and the World Wide Web made documents linkable. A new connectivity problem is now emerging: The object to be connected is a capability-bearing agent that can perceive, reason, invoke tools, or act in the physical world. However, these capabilities remain locked inside platforms, organizations, and runtimes, while existing frameworks, interface protocols, and agent-interconnection efforts still leave open the question of what stable public abstraction can connect agents, humans, and infrastructure as a network architecture problem across organizational and physical domains. This vision and position paper argues for agent network: A reference architecture for capability discovery, connection, and coordination among heterogeneous, autonomous, and potentially self-interested agents. We do not present a complete protocol specification or a performance evaluation. Its central position is that open agent networks need a dual narrow-waist architecture. The Agent Locator Protocol (ALP), surfaced through agent:\/\/&lt;name&gt;, provides the connectivity waist for making capability-bearing participants globally locatable, reachable, and minimally deliverable across substrates and organizations. The Task Semantic Intermediate Representation (TSIR) protocol provides the semantic waist for circulating task intent and success criteria as signable and reusable objects. Around two waists, we propose the Agent Shared Cognition Protocol (ASCP) as a candidate coordination runtime for adjustable group cognition, with blackboard-like workspaces and sedimentation as reference mechanisms for shared working state and experience reuse. Together, these abstractions define Networked Intent Realization (NIR): A research agenda for preserving high-level intent, routing it to capable participants, and translating it into executable, traceable, and accountable collaboration across open agent networks.<\/jats:p>\n                  <\/jats:sec>","container-title":["Tsinghua Science and Technology"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7315096","title":["Multi-Agent Evaluation Framework for Government Affairs Large Language Models: A Case Study on Government Service Hotline Scenarios"],"author":[{"given":"Zengding","family":"Bai","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Shuhui","family":"Ai","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Qihang","family":"Gong","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Dong","family":"Luo","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Tianming","family":"Shao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7315096","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Citizen service hotlines are large-scale citizen-administration interaction systems whose reliability and procedural compliance directly affect public trust. Embedding Large Language Models (LLMs) in such systems raises evaluation questions that static benchmarks and single-agent scoring leave unanswered, because they ignore the administrative logic and multi-stakeholder workflows that define routine operations. This study presents a Multi-Agent Evaluation Framework (MAEF) that audits government-affairs LLMs through three coupled mechanisms, namely compliance gatekeepers encoding domain constraints, heterogeneous agent collaboration simulating administrative roles, and a data-driven feedback loop that turns diagnostic findings into targeted optimization. A case study on intelligent work order distribution, using Yuhu District data from Xiangtan City, shows that a fine-tuned specialist model trades general alignment for domain precision. Coverage-risk curves then translate this trade-off into tiered deployment decisions suitable for grassroots government use. MAEF therefore validates individual models and guides risk-graded, auditable integration of LLM components into public operations systems.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6509861","title":["Reliable and Fast Metro Rescheduling: Large Language Model Agent-Guided Optimization"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-4165-5209","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Shan","family":"HUANG","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-9782-218X","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Yongchao","family":"YE","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-3600-6813","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Avraham","family":"NATAN","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-7480-1764","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Yi","family":"WANG","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Weijian","family":"PAN","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-7696-7566","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Qin","family":"luo","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-0990-5119","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Lishuai","family":"Li","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6509861","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Real-time train timetable rescheduling is constrained by a fundamental trade-off between solution optimality and computational speed. This work introduces an Large Language Model (LLM) Agent-Guided Optimization Framework that produces near-optimal solutions with sub-second latency. The framework integrates a LLM agent with a mathematical solver to overcome the documented limitations of existing methods. Conventional optimization methods, while guaranteeing global optimality, are too computationally intensive for real-time applications. Conversely, heuristics often fail to generalize to new network topologies, reinforcement learning can produce sub-optimal results, and direct LLM applications are limited by physical constraint violations and high inference latency. Our methodology involves fine-tuning an LLM agent on a dataset of high-quality solving traces from an exact solver. This process enables the agent to learn effective solution strategies. During inference, the agent generates strategic configurations\u2014such as warm starts, variable fixings, and branching priorities\u2014which are fed to a Mixed-Integer Linear Programming (MILP) solver. These configurations guide the solver, significantly pruning its search space and accelerating convergence. Experimental results on complex network scenarios show our framework achieves a computational speedup of up to 110-fold compared to a standard MILP baseline, reducing decision latency to under one second. A key finding is that the framework&amp;apos;s inference time is largely decoupled from problem size and complexity. This allows it to maintain near-optimal solution quality in large-scale scenarios where the performance of the baseline solver degrades significantly.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.5220\/0014354400004052","title":["SAT-Based Large Neighborhood Search for Multi-Agent Pathfinding"],"author":[{"given":"Max","family":"Frommknecht","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Information Technology, Czech Technical University in Prague, Th\u00e1kurova 9, Prague, Czechia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Pavel","family":"Surynek","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Faculty of Information Technology, Czech Technical University in Prague, Th\u00e1kurova 9, Prague, Czechia"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5220\/0014354400004052","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 18th International Conference on Agents and Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6293368","title":["Hierarchical Context-Aware Dynamic Memory System for Large Language Model Recommendation with evolving user interests"],"author":[{"given":"Zining","family":"Feng","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Zijing","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Hongzan","family":"Mao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Baisong","family":"Liu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6293368","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The integration of Large Language Models into recommender systems has unlocked new potential in cognitive semantic reasoning and user understanding. However, the efficacy of existing LLM-based approaches in capturing long-term and evolving user preferences is constrained by finite context windows and the lack of structured knowledge representation mechanisms. Prevalent methods typically rely on flat or monolithic memory structures, which suffer from noise accumulation and fail to effectively disentangle transient short-term intents from enduring long-term preferences, particularly within dynamic conversational environments.To address these limitations, we reconceptualize LLM-based recommendation as a hierarchical knowledge modeling problem, positing that user preference evolution requires structured cognitive abstraction rather than mere information storage. To this end, we propose the Hierarchical ContextAware Memory System (HCAMS), a framework that organizes user knowledge into three granularity levels: an Instance Memory Tier (IMT) for preserving episodic behavioral details, a Semantic Aggregation Tier (SAT) for abstracting high-level preference schemas, and a Context-Aware Adaptation Tier (CAAT) that functions as a control policy to regulate memory access based on real-time contextual signals.Distinguishing itself from methods that treat context as auxiliary features,\u00a0HCAMS utilizes context as an active gating mechanism that governs the access to different knowledge granularities. This hierarchical design enables the system to balance short-term responsiveness with long-term preference stability, while improving robustness against preference drift and contextual volatility. By integrating HCAMS with an LLM, the proposed framework effectively decouples memory retention from reasoning, allowing the LLM to perform grounded semantic inference over structured evidence rather than noisy raw sequences.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1109\/ldav68558.2025.00007","title":["ChatVis: Large Language Model Agent for Generating Scientific Visualizations"],"author":[{"given":"Tom","family":"Peterka","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Argonne National Laboratory"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Tanwi","family":"Mallick","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Argonne National Laboratory"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Orcun","family":"Yildiz","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Argonne National Laboratory"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"David","family":"Lenz","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Argonne National Laboratory"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Cory","family":"Quammen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Kitware, Inc."}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Berk","family":"Geveci","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Kitware, Inc."}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/ldav68558.2025.00007","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,2]]},"container-title":["2025 IEEE 15th Symposium on Large Data Analysis and Visualization (LDAV)"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/fllm67465.2025.11391217","title":["Multi-Model Synthetic Training for Mission-Critical Small Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Nolan","family":"Platt","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Virginia Tech,Blacksburg,Virginia"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Pragyansmita","family":"Nayak","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Hitachi Vantara Federal,Reston,Virginia"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/fllm67465.2025.11391217","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,25]]},"container-title":["2025 3rd International Conference on Foundation and Large Language Models (FLLM)"]},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Large language model (LLM)-based multi-agent systems show strong potential for supporting complex reasoning and decision-making in dialogue tasks. However, existing systems often rely on centralized coordination or uncontrolled inter-agent communication, which can limit scalability and increase communication overhead in multi-domain task-oriented dialogue environments. In this paper, we propose a decentralized multi-agent framework that combines role-based collaboration with communication-efficient coordination, enabling agents to operate effectively under constrained token budgets. The proposed approach aims to improve scalability, reduce communication cost, and enhance task success across diverse dialogue domains. Experimental results demonstrate that the proposed method outperforms single-agent and centralized coordination baselines, achieving a 15% improvement in task success rate and a 20% reduction in communication cost. These findings indicate that communication-efficient decentralized coordination significantly enhances both efficiency and robustness. Overall, the proposed architecture provides a practical and scalable solution for multi-domain task-oriented dialogue systems, particularly in resource-constrained environments.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.47772\/ijriss.2026.100601182","title":["Decentralized Collaborative Reasoning with Communication-Constrained Multi-Agent Large Language Models for Multi-Domain Dialogue Systems"],"author":[{"given":"Madhusundar","family":"Nelson","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Saveetha School of Engineering, Saveetha Institute of Medical and Technical Sciences, Chennai, 602105, India."},{"name":"India"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,14]]},"container-title":["International Journal of Research and Innovation in Social Science"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.47772\/ijriss.2026.100601182"},{"DOI":"10.1109\/icip55913.2025.11084359","title":["PDD-AGENT: Multimodal Large Language Model-Driven AI Agent for Enhanced Plant Disease Diagnosis"],"author":[{"given":"Lufu","family":"Qin","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Guizhou University,State Key Laboratory of Public Big Data, College of Computer Science and Technology,Guiyang,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Xingcai","family":"Wu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Guizhou University,State Key Laboratory of Public Big Data, College of Computer Science and Technology,Guiyang,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Xinyu","family":"Dong","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Guizhou University,State Key Laboratory of Public Big Data, College of Computer Science and Technology,Guiyang,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Huan","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Guizhou University,State Key Laboratory of Public Big Data, College of Computer Science and Technology,Guiyang,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Tingwei","family":"Yang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Guizhou University,State Key Laboratory of Public Big Data, College of Computer Science and Technology,Guiyang,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Qi","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Guizhou University,State Key Laboratory of Public Big Data, College of Computer Science and Technology,Guiyang,China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/icip55913.2025.11084359","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,9,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,9,14]]},"container-title":["2025 IEEE International Conference on Image Processing (ICIP)"]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3807246.3807265","title":["MAS-DPD: Multi-Agent System for Dark Pattern Detection Using Large Vision-Language Models"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-8596-5463","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Xiang","family":"Wang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Shenzhen University, Shenzhen, Guangdong, China"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3807246.3807265","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,6]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,5,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,6]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 2026 International Conference on Artificial Intelligence and Control"]},{"DOI":"10.66238\/fsrma115","title":["Graph-MARL-CloudLLM: A Graph-enhanced Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning Framework for Adaptive Resource Scheduling and Workload Optimization of Large Language Model Inference Services across Multi-Cloud Platforms"],"author":[{"family":"Min Qiu Trine","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"family":"Jinxiang Yu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"family":"Ningyun Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.66238\/fsrma115","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,30]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The increasing adoption of Large Language Models (LLMs) in enterprise artificial intelligence applications has created significant challenges for inference service management across multi-cloud platforms, where heterogeneous computing resources, dynamic workloads, network fluctuations, and strict quality-of-service (QoS) requirements hinder efficient resource utilization and stable service delivery. This paper aims to develop an intelligent scheduling framework for cross-cloud LLM inference services that can achieve adaptive workload orchestration, low-latency inference, and cost-efficient resource allocation. We propose Graph-MARL-CloudLLM (Graph-enhanced Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning for Cross-Cloud Large Language Model Inference Optimization), a novel framework that integrates Graph Neural Networks (GNNs) with Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning (MARL) to model complex cloud resource dependencies and perform autonomous scheduling decisions. Specifically, Graph-MARL-CloudLLM constructs a dynamic resource graph containing cloud nodes, GPU clusters, LLM instances, and inference requests, and employs cooperative agents for cloud selection, model selection, GPU allocation, and request routing. A QoS-aware reinforcement learning strategy is further designed to jointly optimize latency, throughput, cost, and resource utilization. Experimental results on a simulated heterogeneous multi-cloud LLM inference environment demonstrate that Graph-MARL-CloudLLM achieves superior scheduling performance compared with existing approaches. Specifically, the proposed framework reduces P99 latency from 271 ms to 194 ms, improves GPU utilization to 86.9%, and decreases inference cost by 17.7% compared with the MARL-based baseline. These results validate the effectiveness of graph-enhanced state representation and cooperative multi-agent optimization for adaptive LLM inference scheduling.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Fundamental Scientific Reports in Multidisciplinary Areas"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/urtc68753.2025.11532975","title":["Decentralized Multi-Agent Goal Assignment for Path Planning Using Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Murad","family":"Ismayilov","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"McGill University,Center for Intelligent Machines (CIM) Laboratory,Montreal,Canada"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Edwin","family":"Meriaux","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"McGill University,Center for Intelligent Machines (CIM) Laboratory,Montreal,Canada"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Shuo","family":"Wen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"McGill University,Center for Intelligent Machines (CIM) Laboratory,Montreal,Canada"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Gregory","family":"Dudek","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"McGill University,Center for Intelligent Machines (CIM) Laboratory,Montreal,Canada"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/urtc68753.2025.11532975","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,10,10]]},"container-title":["2025 IEEE MIT Undergraduate Research Technology Conference (URTC)"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/re63999.2025.00063","title":["Multi-Agent Debate Strategies to Enhance Requirements Engineering with Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Marc","family":"Oriol","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Universitat Polit&#x00E8;cnica de Catalunya,Barcelona,Spain"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Quim","family":"Motger","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Universitat Polit&#x00E8;cnica de Catalunya,Barcelona,Spain"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jordi","family":"Marco","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Universitat Polit&#x00E8;cnica de Catalunya,Barcelona,Spain"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xavier","family":"Franch","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Universitat Polit&#x00E8;cnica de Catalunya,Barcelona,Spain"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/re63999.2025.00063","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,9,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,9,1]]},"container-title":["2025 IEEE 33rd International Requirements Engineering Conference (RE)"]},{"DOI":"10.21203\/rs.3.rs-8929856\/v1","title":["Epistemic Field Theory: Predicting and Governing Hallucination in Large Language Models via Multi-Model Consensus"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0009-2760-6073","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"AZRIL","family":"Hamzah","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Eptim.ai"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6188-9470","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Shasha","family":"Teng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"ASCO EDUTECH"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21203\/rs.3.rs-8929856\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,2,27]]},"abstract":"<title>Abstract<\/title>\n                <p>Large language models hallucinate at rates that undermine reliability in high-stakes applications. Mitigation strategies based on repeated sampling or majority voting implicitly assume error independence across samples. We introduce Epistemic Field Theory (EFT), which predicts hallucination probability from multi-model consensus. EFT defines a consensus field \u03c3 \u2208 [0, 1] over query space and derives the predictor P(H) = (1\u2009\u2212\u2009\u03c3)\u00b7\u03b7, where \u03b7 is a model-specific noise coefficient. Across 13,728 human-validated responses (Cohen\u2019s \u03ba\u2009=\u20090.87) from four frontier models in three professional domains, \u03c3 predicts hallucination with AUC\u2009=\u20090.787, outperforming majority voting (0.518), SelfCheck methods (0.358\u20130.377), and self-reported confidence (0.461). Hallucination counts exhibit systematic overdispersion (\u03c1\u2009=\u20091.50), with empirical majority-failure rates 2.96\u00d7 higher than independence predicts. Epistemic grounding reduces hallucination rates but not error correlation, revealing frequency and structure as independent dimensions of the hallucination problem.<\/p>"},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6998159","title":["&lt;p&gt;AgenTrade: An Engineering Case Study in Selective Language-Model Use for a Multi-Agent Trading System&lt;\/p&gt;"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2558-7149","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Daniele","family":"Ferrante","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6998159","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>&lt;p&gt;AgenTrade is a multi-agent algorithmic trading system that produces trade decisions across foreign-exchange, metals, index, energy, cryptocurrency, and micro-futures markets. It is built on a single deliberate principle: a large language model is used only where the task is genuinely open-ended reasoning over ambiguous text, and deterministic code is used everywhere else. This paper is an engineering case study of that principle in practice. It documents the system's architecture, the design decisions that shaped it, and the measured effect of moving work out of language models and into code: a fall in monthly inference cost from roughly $501 to $17 to 27, achieved together with, not at the expense of, determinism, latency, and auditability. The paper also reports a set of production failures and their fixes, several of them silent failures in which the system kept producing confident output from a broken input, and distils the patterns that recur across them, including the distinction between binary flags and weighted scores, the divergence between broker state and client state, and caching on the cadence of the information rather than of the request. The contribution is not a new model or a claimed trading edge; it is a worked, honest example of where the language model should and should not sit in a production multi-agent system.&lt;\/p&gt;<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.3389\/frai.2025.1771737","title":["Correction: Multi-agent systems powered by large language models: applications in swarm intelligence"],"author":[{"given":"Cristian","family":"Jimenez-Romero","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"ETIS Laboratory, ENSEA, CNRS, UMR8051, CY Cergy-Paris University","place":["Cergy, France"]}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Alper","family":"Yegenoglu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Independent Researcher","place":["J\u00fclich, Germany"]}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Christian","family":"Blum","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Artificial Intelligence Research Institute (IIIA-CSIC)","place":["Bellaterra, Spain"]}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3389\/frai.2025.1771737","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,1,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,1,22]]},"container-title":["Frontiers in Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/s42979-026-05242-8","title":["A Multi-Agent Benchmarking Framework for Evaluating the Performance of Large Language Models in Logic Programming"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-1842-7366","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Nikolaos","family":"Karamousalidis","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-6846-7374","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Petros","family":"Kefalas","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/s42979-026-05242-8","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,30]]},"container-title":["SN Computer Science"]},{"DOI":"10.21203\/rs.3.rs-7483304\/v1","title":["Large Language Model Agent for Modular TaskExecution in Drug Discovery"],"author":[{"given":"Janghoon","family":"Ock","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Carnegie Mellon University"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Radheesh Sharma","family":"Meda","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Carnegie Mellon University"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Srivathsan","family":"Badrinarayanan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Carnegie Mellon University"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Neha S.","family":"Aluru","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Texas A&M University"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Achuth","family":"Chandrasekhar","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Carnegie Mellon University"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Amir Barati","family":"Farimani","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Carnegie Mellon University"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21203\/rs.3.rs-7483304\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,9,17]]},"abstract":"<title>Abstract<\/title>\n        <p>We present a modular framework powered by large language models (LLMs) that automates and streamlines key tasks across the early-stage computational drug discovery pipeline. By combining LLM reasoning with domain-specific tools, the framework performs biomedical data retrieval, domain-specific question answering, molecular generation, property prediction, property-aware molecular refinement, and 3D protein\u2013ligand structure generation. In a case study targeting BCL-2 in lymphocytic leukemia, the agent autonomously retrieved relevant biomolecular information\u2014including FASTA sequences, SMILES representations, and literature\u2014and answered mechanistic questions with improved contextual accuracy over standard LLMs. It then generated chemically diverse seed molecules and predicted 67 ADMET-related properties, which guided iterative molecular refinement. Across two refinement rounds, the number of molecules with QED &gt; 0.6 increased from 34 to 55, and those passing at least four out of five empirical drug-likeness rules rose from 29 to 52, within a pool of 194 molecules. The framework also employed Boltz-2 to generate 3D protein\u2013ligand complexes and provide rapid binding affinity estimates for candidate compounds. These results demonstrate that the approach effectively supports molecular screening, prioritization, and structure evaluation. Its modular design enables flexible integration of evolving tools and models, providing a scalable foundation for AI-assisted therapeutic discovery.<\/p>"}],"items-per-page":100,"query":{"start-index":100,"search-terms":null}}}